{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why","id":1477121,"title":"What do you like better, D&D 5e or WoD, and why?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-03-23T14:53:32-07:00","id":1477121,"title":"What do you like better, D&D 5e or WoD, and why?","content":"I like both, depending on my mood and what friend group I\u2019m playing with.<br /> <br /> I thought about asking this on the D&amp;D forum, but the impression I get is that very few of the regulars there have ever played WoD, let alone Trinity Continuum (my other fave).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T15:45:52-07:00","id":1477127,"title":"","content":"Depends which WoD we're talking about? It seems impossible to compare all of the WoD to one edition of D&amp;D.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T15:55:14-07:00","id":1477128,"title":"","content":"Let\u2019s say V5, since that\u2019s the most likely to have an overlapping fan base with D&amp;D 5e. Or if you hate V5 you can use the 20th line.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T16:33:27-07:00","id":1477130,"title":"","content":"I hope that only having played up to 3.5 D&amp;D, I'm still qualified to answer. I will just go with recent (I mean very recent) experience. I was just asked, &quot;When can we play Mage?&quot;. A storyteller in a WoD game has one major challenge. Almost every character that the players encounter is going to be some version of human. The creative work required to generate a setting populated with believable human-like characters is, I would say, considerable. There are ways to direct the chronicle so that you maximize focus on the characters you put your effort into and enjoy roleplaying, without railroading the players. It's a fine line and demands attention to your craft. Another challenge - the 10th Sphere in Mage - is guns, in a system with seven health levels.<br /> <br /> There is a lot more written content detailing monsters in D&amp;D. Goblins have a much lower roleplay-performance requirement and can be repeatedly killed by PCs who gain more HP as they level. One of the best D&amp;D campaigns I ran came to me after we had switched to WoD for a long time and then switched back. I had been talking with my British friend who's a history buff. Why did people in a medieval society chose not to commit crime when video-cameras didn't exist? It was often because they believed God would punish them.<br /> <br /> In a D&amp;D village, you have witness testimony as the main form of evidence and, at beginning levels, not a whole lot of Divination spells readily available. What happens when there are social fault lines and people willing to lie? The plot I used was really fun. A group of bandits with money from previous plunder came to a city where many of the men had been killed and married into the local population, sometimes becoming adoptive fathers. They had intended to refrain from banditry, but old habits die hard, some carrying on and recruiting the young. There were good-aligned temples with radically different views. Add a vast wilderness with a complex monster ecology and you get a campaign that had my phone ringing off the hook.<br /> <br /> Many years later, I have returned to Mage for the philosophical depth of the Ascension War and the boundless possibilities of the Umbra. I love the Technocracy, how it's specialized Conventions and Methodologies fit together. Murder, sex, intrigue and the occasional monster. If I'm ever at a loss for a new conflict, my fallback is, &quot;Mentor's demands conflict with Avatar's Seeking&quot;. If there is one monster from D&amp;D I think fits well into Mage, it's the Aboleth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thank you for passing time with me in conversation. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480242-horizon-s-wild-and-crazy-homebrew-rules-and-metaplot-choices\" target=\"_blank\">My Hacks</a>.","edited":{"userid":26467,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26467-horizonparty2021","user":"HorizonParty2021","date":". 03-24-2022, 09:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26467-horizonparty2021","name":"HorizonParty2021","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26467_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T17:50:44-07:00","id":1477141,"title":"","content":"V20 &gt; D&amp;D 5e &gt; V5 for me.<br /> <br /> If I'm playing cWoD, the 20th books still hit what I want out of them the most (though I prefer CofD in general too). <br /> <br /> WoD and D&amp;D scratch very different itches in gaming though. D&amp;D 5e is... well... kinda bland to me. It works, it's fun, it's easy to get into, I'm happy to get away from the 3.5/Pathfinder 1e mechanical design philosophy, but the end result leaves me wanting flavor that isn't there (though I can find it in 5e based games like Pugmire or Legendlore). I'm really looking forward to ICON from Massif Press (makers of Lancer) that might be the D&amp;D 4e that works because it's not D&amp;D and can lean into the mechanical cleanliness 4e offered. There are just so many not--D&amp;D games out there that do D&amp;D style games I find way more fun.<br /> <br /> V5 leaves me cold.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T18:06:23-07:00","id":1477144,"title":"","content":"Thank you guys.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T20:58:49-07:00","id":1477161,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477141#post1477141\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">V20 &gt; D&amp;D 5e &gt; V5 for me.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's about it for me too. V5 feels like D&amp;D 4e to me, which I also didn't enjoy or play.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T23:27:30-07:00","id":1477173,"title":"","content":"I don't think D&amp;D 5th ed as bland as Heavy Arms do, but it is really hard to compare two games so different. As a rule of thumb I prefer CoD to WoD, too, although my gaming group unfortunately does not. From WoD I still like Changeling and Demon enough to stick to the classic, although not ditching the CoD versions, but simply treating as completely different games, too.<br /> <br /> D&amp;D covers so many game styles I like. But I have to agree, 5th ed still has its problems even if it mostly divorced from the worse aspects of 4th and 3rd. Still, I love this game just as much as CoD and my actual preference depends on my mood.<br /> <br /> There are other games, of course. I really love the setting of L5R and I have a long game currently in hiatus using the setting on the D&amp;D 3.5 rules, although it demands a lot of compromise (L5R rules set <i>works</i>, but up to 4th edition that's the best you can say about it). D&amp;D-likes never did it for me better than the original, though, usually because I have severe disagreements with the authors on what was wrong with D&amp;D.<br /> <br /> Setting-wise, Ravenloft 3.5 hits the sweet spot like none other. I can run it in any system whatsoever, mix with any other game, I simply love it above any other setting. As an ST I already stranded a Technocratic ship there to see how they fare. It is delicious. And D&amp;D has the benefit of, by ditching our world from the equation, offering a greater variety of places to explore themes, genres and game styles. But Ravenloft is like no other.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T01:40:50-07:00","id":1477181,"title":"","content":"D&amp;D's blandness is, in many ways, a considerable strength. It's a canvas that you can paint masterpieces on. There are tons of great flavor (from individual antagonist blocks to full settings) people make for D&amp;D. The blandness of its core nature are what allows for so much great third party stuff to get added to it without having to compete with as much existing stuff. Bland in the sense I mean it, also means a ton of functionality for it as a game. <br /> <br /> It's like when car companies make a muscle car, or a hot hatch. The stock models are bland, but popular precisely because of how easy they are (very intentionally now) to modify into something awesome (and it lowers the bar for entry since they tend to be relatively cheap up front and you can kit out as you get spending money to do so).<br /> <br /> AD&amp;D 2e, 3e, and 4e all had more flavor, but at the cost of quirks that made them less flexible than 5e is. Some people love those quirks because that's what they wanted in the first place, so they're very loyal to those editions. Other people get really bothered by those quirks and stick with an older one/switch to the next one after/move on to a different system altogether. 5e seems to have found that sweet spot where it doesn't have too many quirks pushing it towards the extremes of the previous editions. 5e is exceedingly popular for it IMO. But for me, I know what I want out of a D&amp;D game, so I don't need a blank canvas to play with until I find it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T04:10:56-07:00","id":1477189,"title":"","content":"I will take any vampire product (CoD first then revised, V20 and lastly V5) over DnD 5th any day, the older versions on the other hand like B\\X and other retroclones like Old School Essentials or Whitehack, I would say that they come second after CoD, I just think that they have more depth and I especially like the problem solving attitude over 5th edition &quot;kill monsters for xp&quot; stuff.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27142-newb95","name":"Newb95","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"502"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T05:55:54-07:00","id":1477196,"title":"","content":"I think I prefer WoD, if the D&amp;D game I'm currently working on is any indication. It was supposed to be a brief average high fantasy adventure, and now it's a morally ambiguous, macabre dark fantasy thing looking like something out of the Labyrinth (complete with Oblivion/The Tempest).<br /> Half of my plots, half way through the premise I suddenly realise; &quot;Wait, this is a Vampire/Wraith/Changeling plot.&quot;<br /> <br /> But D&amp;D has the advantage that I can go totally freeform and make it up as I go. WoD requires research and has less flexibility, because its setting mirrors reality and has its own rules. Its easier to write in D&amp;D, because I feel less... pressure? To conform with what the gamelines tell me I should be doing (20th anniversary is not as bad about this. V5, unortunately for me, brought back the metaplot)<br /> <br /> So I guess I prefer WoD for the setting... I prefer WoD for the basics of the setting, but I enjoy D&amp;D's toolset nature.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T06:59:07-07:00","id":1477199,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477173#post1477173\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think D&amp;D 5th ed as bland as Heavy Arms do, but it is really hard to compare two games so different. As a rule of thumb I prefer CoD to WoD, too, although my gaming group unfortunately does not. From WoD I still like Changeling and Demon enough to stick to the classic, although not ditching the CoD versions, but simply treating as completely different games, too.<br /> <br /> D&amp;D covers so many game styles I like. But I have to agree, 5th ed still has its problems even if it mostly divorced from the worse aspects of 4th and 3rd. Still, I love this game just as much as CoD and my actual preference depends on my mood.<br /> <br /> There are other games, of course. I really love the setting of L5R and I have a long game currently in hiatus using the setting on the D&amp;D 3.5 rules, although it demands a lot of compromise (L5R rules set <i>works</i>, but up to 4th edition that's the best you can say about it). D&amp;D-likes never did it for me better than the original, though, usually because I have severe disagreements with the authors on what was wrong with D&amp;D.<br /> <br /> Setting-wise, Ravenloft 3.5 hits the sweet spot like none other. I can run it in any system whatsoever, mix with any other game, I simply love it above any other setting. As an ST I already stranded a Technocratic ship there to see how they fare. It is delicious. And D&amp;D has the benefit of, by ditching our world from the equation, offering a greater variety of places to explore themes, genres and game styles. But Ravenloft is like no other.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ravenloft is pretty cool. But I never read any of the older Ravenloft stuff, just Van Richten\u2019s Guide to Ravenloft for 5e.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T07:01:12-07:00","id":1477200,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Newb95</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477189#post1477189\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I will take any vampire product (CoD first then revised, V20 and lastly V5) over DnD 5th any day, the older versions on the other hand like B\\X and other retroclones like Old School Essentials or Whitehack, I would say that they come second after CoD, I just think that they have more depth and I especially like the problem solving attitude over 5th edition &amp;quot;kill monsters for xp&amp;quot; stuff.</div> </div> </div> </div>I disagree with your analysis. 5e offers the milestone method of leveling up, which I personally use for my campaigns and which is much better than killing monsters for xp, the default method in earlier editions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T07:03:33-07:00","id":1477201,"title":"","content":"One of my players has Correspondence 3 and the ability to teleport doesn't cause any problems in the game. We can get on Google Earth and be pretty accurate about the local geography of a place I haven't been. The little bit of paradox that accumulates each time is enough to limit their use of it.<br /> <br /> When I ran D&amp;D, I would plan things so that by the time someone got teleport, the party was so tied up in intrigue and sworn obligations that the story remained centered on the places I was familiar with. &quot;Sure, you can retreat to a secluded mountain to recuperate, but the Duchess still needs help to survive a coup attempt.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thank you for passing time with me in conversation. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480242-horizon-s-wild-and-crazy-homebrew-rules-and-metaplot-choices\" target=\"_blank\">My Hacks</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26467-horizonparty2021","name":"HorizonParty2021","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26467_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T07:04:23-07:00","id":1477202,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rhywbeth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477196#post1477196\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think I prefer WoD, if the D&amp;D game I'm currently working on is any indication. It was supposed to be a brief average high fantasy adventure, and now it's a morally ambiguous, macabre dark fantasy thing looking like something out of the Labyrinth (complete with Oblivion/The Tempest).<br /> Half of my plots, half way through the premise I suddenly realise; &amp;quot;Wait, this is a Vampire/Wraith/Changeling plot.&amp;quot;<br /> <br /> But D&amp;D has the advantage that I can go totally freeform and make it up as I go. WoD requires research and has less flexibility, because its setting mirrors reality and has its own rules. Its easier to write in D&amp;D, because I feel less... pressure? To conform with what the gamelines tell me I should be doing (20th anniversary is not as bad about this. V5, unortunately for me, brought back the metaplot)<br /> <br /> So I guess I prefer WoD for the setting... I prefer WoD for the basics of the setting, but I enjoy D&amp;D's toolset nature.</div> </div> </div> </div>Right now I prefer 5e, but I do use a lot of moral ambiguity and dark fantasy elements that come straight from the time I spent playing WoD with a different friend group. However, though WoD was the first WW/OP game I ever played, overall I prefer the brighter more heroic (but still sometimes tragic) tone of Trinity Continuum.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T07:05:24-07:00","id":1477203,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HorizonParty2021</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477201#post1477201\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One of my players has Correspondence 3 and the ability to teleport doesn't cause any problems in the game. We can get on Google Earth and be pretty accurate about the local geography of a place I haven't been. The little bit of paradox that accumulates each time is enough to limit their use of it.<br /> <br /> When I ran D&amp;D, I would plan things so that by the time someone got teleport, the party was so tied up in intrigue and sworn obligations that the story remained centered on the places I was familiar with. &amp;quot;Sure, you can retreat to a secluded mountain to recuperate, but the Duchess still needs help to survive a coup attempt.&amp;quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm. That makes sense. Thanks \ud83d\ude0a.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T07:17:08-07:00","id":1477206,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477181#post1477181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But for me, I know what I want out of a D&amp;D game, so I don't need a blank canvas to play with until I find it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair, indeed, and mostly agree with your take. For me, though, the things I don't get from the system itself already I get from the settings. To be honest I never found a D&amp;D-like that really did what I wanted, so I'm working on my own, but 5e did a nice job. And the versatility is important to me because it lets me explore several settings, so the &quot;bland&quot; (quoted because I don't personally use the word in the same sense, but I'm using yours) aspect is important. Being able to explore Greyhawk, Darksun and Ravenloft with the same ease is important to me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Newb95</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477189#post1477189\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I just think that they have more depth and I especially like the problem solving attitude over 5th edition &quot;kill monsters for xp&quot; stuff.</div> </div> </div> </div>I find it always a curious comment. Older editions literally have only two rules for leveling up, kill things for XP or grab money for XP. 3e started to explore other options and really make room for other problem-solving methods being in the rules instead of just improvised in the adventure modules, and 4th and 5th editions improved on those.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T07:26:43-07:00","id":1477209,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477200#post1477200\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I disagree with your analysis. 5e offers the milestone method of leveling up, which I personally use for my campaigns and which is much better than killing monsters for xp, the default method in earlier editions.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Killing monsters was the worse possible methods of gaining xp and often resulted in players getting one shoted by lucky rolls, finding treasures was the foremost method of gaining xp and the only thing that actually mattered, you have a room full of goblins blocking your way to the treasure? sure go ahead and try to kill all of them at level 1, see how long you last or you could play it smart and negotiate with them, sneak around them, trick them into running into a trap you avoided before and so on, creative problem solving is the thing I most love about older dnd, characters are fragile and even by leveling up, you still remain relatively squishy so playing it smart was always the best course of action.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27142-newb95","name":"Newb95","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"502"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T07:29:50-07:00","id":1477210,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477206#post1477206\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Fair, indeed, and mostly agree with your take. For me, though, the things I don't get from the system itself already I get from the settings. To be honest I never found a D&amp;D-like that really did what I wanted, so I'm working on my own, but 5e did a nice job. And the versatility is important to me because it lets me explore several settings, so the &quot;bland&quot; (quoted because I don't personally use the word in the same sense, but I'm using yours) aspect is important. Being able to explore Greyhawk, Darksun and Ravenloft with the same ease is important to me.<br /> <br /> <br /> I find it always a curious comment. Older editions literally have only two rules for leveling up, kill things for XP or grab money for XP. 3e started to explore other options and really make room for other problem-solving methods being in the rules instead of just improvised in the adventure modules, and 4th and 5th editions improved on those.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Creative problem solving was built into the game from the beginning, 3e just codified it better, running into a dungeon thinking you are the one with the biggest dick was a surefire way to end up dead, improvising solutions and thinking outside the box was always the best thing to do especially when special monsters like undeads and werewolves started showing up since those things only get damaged by silver weapons and holy water, stuff that you most likely won't have at ready when you need it most hence one more reason to be creative.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27142-newb95","name":"Newb95","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"502"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T07:45:11-07:00","id":1477212,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Newb95</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477210#post1477210\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Creative problem solving was built into the game from the beginning, 3e just codified it better, running into a dungeon thinking you are the one with the biggest dick was a surefire way to end up dead, improvising solutions and thinking outside the box was always the best thing to do especially when special monsters like undeads and werewolves started showing up since those things only get damaged by silver weapons and holy water, stuff that you most likely won't have at ready when you need it most hence one more reason to be creative.</div> </div> </div> </div>Got it, but how do you think 5e lessens that at all?<br /> <br /> Having tables of XP per monster? AD&amp;D have that all the same, it has been the main gauge of how much XP to give since a long time ago, but it is only a gauge, you don't have to literally kill anything for that, unless the DM decides so (and such DMs decided so even more frequently in the past).<br /> <br /> I mean, you don't like 5e because you only value how Basic did XP?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T08:14:29-07:00","id":1477214,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477212#post1477212\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Got it, but how do you think 5e lessens that at all?<br /> <br /> Having tables of XP per monster? AD&amp;D have that all the same, it has been the main gauge of how much XP to give since a long time ago, but it is only a gauge, you don't have to literally kill anything for that, unless the DM decides so (and such DMs decided so even more frequently in the past).<br /> <br /> I mean, you don't like 5e because you only value how Basic did XP?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I don't like 5e because it turned into a power fantasy, you gotta put some actual effort to actually get killed in that game and the intricated backstory for characters with bonuses and powergaming galore basically turned it into another thing entirely. In the original dnd you didn't get attached too much to characters since they were most likely going to die horribly anyway, once you leveled up a little, got some magic items and generally upped your chances of survival then you could start thinking about developing your characters backstory a little more if you bothered at all with it, the PCs were not the centerpiece of the game, it was the dungeon, the traps, the treasures, the dangers and how you dealt with all of that, the characters were all adventurers ready to risk their lives for treasure, that was all the thinking you needed to put into your backstory.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27142-newb95","name":"Newb95","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"502"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T09:53:02-07:00","id":1477226,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Newb95</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477214#post1477214\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I don't like 5e because it turned into a power fantasy, you gotta put some actual effort to actually get killed in that game and the intricated backstory for characters with bonuses and powergaming galore basically turned it into another thing entirely. In the original dnd you didn't get attached too much to characters since they were most likely going to die horribly anyway, once you leveled up a little, got some magic items and generally upped your chances of survival then you could start thinking about developing your characters backstory a little more if you bothered at all with it, the PCs were not the centerpiece of the game, it was the dungeon, the traps, the treasures, the dangers and how you dealt with all of that, the characters were all adventurers ready to risk their lives for treasure, that was all the thinking you needed to put into your backstory.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am exactly the opposite. I love all the love and effort you can put into your character\u2019s backstory in 5e. I think PCs should be the center of the game and death should be rare, to such an extent that in my private notes for one of the 5e campaigns I\u2019m involved in I call the campaign \u201cThe Lyra Chronicles\u201d after my PC. Cause from my perspective the campaign is the story of Lyra\u2019s life and the lives of her friends in the party.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T10:35:34-07:00","id":1477231,"title":"","content":"I believe the love of older D&amp;D being the TTRPG predecessor and equivalent to the Souls games is one of those &quot;quirks&quot; I mentioned. Some people love how much certain editions of D&amp;D are meat grinders that will chew up and spit out disposable PCs as you master the game. Some people hate it because it disincentivizes getting attached to your characters.<br /> <br /> The debates of whether D&amp;D should be &quot;easier&quot; so people that don't want that high-death game play loop can get into the game mirror the debates about the same in From Software's game designs. D&amp;D 5e absolutely ditched the idea of it (to the chagrin of people that like it) but it has proven the most accessible and popular edition of D&amp;D yet.... so... the OSR movement has made dozens of retroclones.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T14:20:25-07:00","id":1477269,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477226#post1477226\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I am exactly the opposite. I love all the love and effort you can put into your character\u2019s backstory in 5e. I think PCs should be the center of the game and death should be rare, to such an extent that in my private notes for one of the 5e campaigns I\u2019m involved in I call the campaign \u201cThe Lyra Chronicles\u201d after my PC. Cause from my perspective the campaign is the story of Lyra\u2019s life and the lives of her friends in the party.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> To each their own, I'm not spending hours writing a small essay about my dragonoid paladin or whatever to play in a game I know I will basically never die unless I pull off something incredibly stupid, I like the risk, the tension and the all or nothing feeling you get from old school dnd and given that the OSR scene is thriving right now and I never have trouble finding games, I bet the OSR market will grow even more, If only CoD had such a boost in popularity...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27142-newb95","name":"Newb95","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"502"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T14:35:55-07:00","id":1477273,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477231#post1477231\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I believe the love of older D&amp;D being the TTRPG predecessor and equivalent to the Souls games is one of those &quot;quirks&quot; I mentioned. Some people love how much certain editions of D&amp;D are meat grinders that will chew up and spit out disposable PCs as you master the game. Some people hate it because it disincentivizes getting attached to your characters.<br /> <br /> The debates of whether D&amp;D should be &quot;easier&quot; so people that don't want that high-death game play loop can get into the game mirror the debates about the same in From Software's game designs. D&amp;D 5e absolutely ditched the idea of it (to the chagrin of people that like it) but it has proven the most accessible and popular edition of D&amp;D yet.... so... the OSR movement has made dozens of retroclones.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Some OSR titles like White hack and lamentations of the flame princess tried to lower the mortality rate by not having characters die when they hit 0 hp only when they go into -3 or something, I'm not necessarily against that because it can help new players and not drive them away from the game (When I used to run some B\\X, I regularly fudged the rolls of new players to not have them die instantly) while still keeping lethality and risk high.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27142-newb95","name":"Newb95","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"502"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T15:10:29-07:00","id":1477274,"title":"","content":"As I was learning to take the reigns as DM from the more experienced DM who wanted a break, I was faced with an insistence on an aspect of &quot;XP for monsters killed&quot;. If a creature retreats from combat for any reason, the characters get XP for defeating it, even if it was simply because the creature had reason not to kill the PCs. As long as they believe they faced it and forced it to run, they will expect to gain its XP value. I thought about this. They couldn't gain XP from the same creature twice. Some creatures have level-drain abilities.<br /> <br /> It was a long time ago, so the details are a bit vague, but I had a situation where they didn't stand to gain anything by attacking it and risked being level-drained. That allowed some roleplay between them and the creature that would not have been possible had I not thought through to find a way to subvert the antiquated XP system. We started adding milestone XP sometime later.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thank you for passing time with me in conversation. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480242-horizon-s-wild-and-crazy-homebrew-rules-and-metaplot-choices\" target=\"_blank\">My Hacks</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26467-horizonparty2021","name":"HorizonParty2021","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26467_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T15:20:15-07:00","id":1477275,"title":"","content":"Regardless of any mechanical tricks... LotFP is probably not something that does anything to help the OSR movement or get new players into old school games at this point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T15:25:03-07:00","id":1477276,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477275#post1477275\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Regardless of any mechanical tricks... LotFP is probably not something that does anything to help the OSR movement or get new players into old school games at this point.</div> </div> </div> </div>I see a lot of LotFP games around and many listings online, the modules in particular are great.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27142-newb95","name":"Newb95","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"502"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-25T03:15:14-07:00","id":1477320,"title":"","content":"I understand the desire for actually dangerous adventures, but I find it more fulfilling to do so by hacking a little 5th edition than playing other games or really old and obnoxious editions (anything pre-3rd). Exploring the dungeon for the sake of it has its own merits, but I don't like so much the idea of not having actual investment on the character. By the way, having an investment doesn't mean to write a small essay, you're mostly just being a jerk with this particular comment.<br /> <br /> My own preferred style is actually low fantasy, and the idea that the PCs are relatively normal people in terms of power, facing off dangerous situations, so I relate to some of your tastes. Yet this <b>is</b> a power fantasy, and high-risk dungeon crawling isn't less of it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-25T08:09:34-07:00","id":1477330,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477320#post1477320\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I understand the desire for actually dangerous adventures, but I find it more fulfilling to do so by hacking a little 5th edition than playing other games or really old and obnoxious editions (anything pre-3rd). Exploring the dungeon for the sake of it has its own merits, but I don't like so much the idea of not having actual investment on the character. <b>By the way, having an investment doesn't mean to write a small essay, you're mostly just being a jerk with this particular comment.</b><br /> <br /> My own preferred style is actually low fantasy, and the idea that the PCs are relatively normal people in terms of power, facing off dangerous situations, so I relate to some of your tastes. Yet this <b>is</b> a power fantasy, and high-risk dungeon crawling isn't less of it.</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#FF0000\"><b>Insults are prohibited. Take a warning please.</b></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Newb95</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477269#post1477269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To each their own, I'm not spending hours writing a small essay about my dragonoid paladin or whatever to play in a game I know I will basically never die unless I pull off something incredibly stupid, I like the risk, the tension and the all or nothing feeling you get from old school dnd and given that the OSR scene is thriving right now and I never have trouble finding games, I bet the OSR market will grow even more, If only CoD had such a boost in popularity...</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#FF0000\"><b>Please do not mock other players' playstyles.</b></span><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 04-01-2022, 06:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-26T12:05:22-07:00","id":1477459,"title":"","content":"One last comment about this thread<br /> <br /> As I said, I like both V5 and D&amp;D 5e. But V5 as a game seems to lead to an ever bleaker and more nihilistic future, while 5e seems bright and full of hope. I like both play styles depending on my mood, but lately I just feel like 5e speaks more to me than V5 does. Don\u2019t get me wrong, I like V5 a lot and I\u2019d love to actually play sometime, but I guess 5e is more like what I need in my life right now. Thanks for helping me figure this out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":26996,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","user":"Amethyst","date":". 03-26-2022, 03:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-01T15:38:41-07:00","id":1477869,"title":"","content":"I generally prefer WOD but I keep returning to the Forgotten Realms over the decades.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-02T20:30:31-07:00","id":1477937,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477869#post1477869\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I generally prefer WOD but I keep returning to the Forgotten Realms over the decades.</div> </div> </div> </div>I love FR! Most of my adventures are set in 5e Faerun (though I\u2019m planning one set on Exandria the Critical Role world rn).<br /> <br /> Do you think Faerun and WoD share any thematic similarities or are they total opposites?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-02T21:20:20-07:00","id":1477939,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477937#post1477937\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I love FR! Most of my adventures are set in 5e Faerun (though I\u2019m planning one set on Exandria the Critical Role world rn).<br /> <br /> Do you think Faerun and WoD share any thematic similarities or are they total opposites?</div> </div> </div> </div>Oddly, I think the World of Darkness helped me appreciate the Forgotten Realms as envisioned by Ed Greenwood more. One of the things I didn't understand as a teenager was that Ed Greenwood's idea for the Realms wasn't a stereotypical Good vs. Evil world but that the forces of good were greatly outmatched and had to play the forces of evil against one another.<br /> <br /> In Ed's world, the reason the Zhentarim and the Red Wizards of Thay and later the Shadovar haven't taken over the world was because they hate each other more than they do the side of GoodTM. Basically, the &quot;Blood War&quot; principle. That didn't make much sense to me until I had exposure to Vampire: The Masquerade and its varying shades of black. It also helped me think about how good has its own struggles and now in my games, the Paladins want to wipe out those damned Harpers and their terrorist Chaotic Good activities and vice versa.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-02T23:53:02-07:00","id":1477942,"title":"","content":"I always liked to add shades of grey to D&amp;D even before WoD, but I don't really like FR, I always preferred good old Greyhawk for the more traditional D&amp;D style. Curiously, a place where Order vs Chaos was more prominent. Ravenloft also plays a nice twist by making Good vs Evil even more important and constant than normal, but also making it more abstract, something the characters can't define in concrete terms as even Outsiders are exceedingly rare and poorly understood.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-03T07:13:55-07:00","id":1477948,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477939#post1477939\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It also helped me think about how good has its own struggles and now in my games, the Paladins want to wipe out those damned Harpers and their terrorist Chaotic Good activities and vice versa.</div> </div> </div> </div>That was one thing I always liked in Pathfinder. You had the Order of the Godclaw, which was an alliance formed by the followers of Lawful gods (Lawful Good, Lawful Neutral and Lawful Evil) to combat the forces of Chaos. The Godclaws followers were mainly Hellknights, but there were even some Paladins and Orders (like the Order of the Torrent) that didn't allow Evil members to join. So it served as an excuse to occasionally have Lawful Good individuals, including Paladins, pop up opposing Chaotic Good countries and organizations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-03T18:46:50-07:00","id":1477979,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477942#post1477942\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I always liked to add shades of grey to D&amp;D even before WoD, but I don't really like FR, I always preferred good old Greyhawk for the more traditional D&amp;D style. Curiously, a place where Order vs Chaos was more prominent. Ravenloft also plays a nice twist by making Good vs Evil even more important and constant than normal, but also making it more abstract, something the characters can't define in concrete terms as even Outsiders are exceedingly rare and poorly understood.</div> </div> </div> </div>I prefer FR to Greyhawk because in general I like high fantasy better than low fantasy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-03T18:48:26-07:00","id":1477980,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477942#post1477942\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I always liked to add shades of grey to D&amp;D even before WoD, but I don't really like FR, I always preferred good old Greyhawk for the more traditional D&amp;D style. Curiously, a place where Order vs Chaos was more prominent. Ravenloft also plays a nice twist by making Good vs Evil even more important and constant than normal, but also making it more abstract, something the characters can't define in concrete terms as even Outsiders are exceedingly rare and poorly understood.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was a big Ravenloft fanboy in the Nineties and even submitted for the Book of Souls a couple of times, though I wasn't yet trained as a writer by college and practice. However, I always felt Ravenloft had a lot of the White Wolf issues that they were terrified of player characters changing the world so they made the Darklords immortal.<br /> <br /> If I were to ever run another Ravenloft campaign, I'd make it clear that the Darklords have NO special protections and that if you kill one, they die. Their domain just becomes part of someone else's domain or passed onto the Prime Material Plane.<br /> <br /> No resurrective immortality.<br /> <br /> Why? Because that way the PCs can actually make the Demiplane of Dread better.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-03T19:24:55-07:00","id":1477983,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477980#post1477980\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was a big Ravenloft fanboy in the Nineties and even submitted for the Book of Souls a couple of times, though I wasn't yet trained as a writer by college and practice. However, I always felt Ravenloft had a lot of the White Wolf issues that they were terrified of player characters changing the world so they made the Darklords immortal.<br /> <br /> If I were to ever run another Ravenloft campaign, I'd make it clear that the Darklords have NO special protections and that if you kill one, they die. Their domain just becomes part of someone else's domain or passed onto the Prime Material Plane.<br /> <br /> No resurrective immortality.<br /> <br /> Why? Because that way the PCs can actually make the Demiplane of Dread better.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was under the impression that's how it always worked, or at least usually worked? There are domains like Gundarak, where Darklords died. The ruler died, was replaced by a new ruler, then that guy died and the domain ended up becoming part of Barovia and Borca because nobody in the kingdom was able to assume the position of the new Darklord. Or Richemulot which has had a series of wererat leaders (The original Darklord, Claude Renier was murdered and replaced by his daughter, and then she was murdered and Jaqueline inherited the position of Darklord). Or Sithicus, where Lord Soth left and was replaced by Inza, the new Darklord.<br /> <br /> Now here are some Darklords who die and then come back, like Azalin, but then he's a spooky lich and coming back from death is just something liches do. And then sometimes you have weird things like OG Mordenheim (Adam is the Darklord and Mordenheim can't die as long as Adam exists). Really I think Strahd is the only exception where he can't be permanently killed, which is more due to him being the foundational cornerstone for the Demiplane of Dread and as a result he's basically a &quot;Super Vampire&quot; with some unique capabilities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":741,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","user":"AnubisXy","date":". 04-03-2022, 10:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-03T23:57:19-07:00","id":1477991,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477983#post1477983\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I was under the impression that's how it always worked, or at least usually worked? There are domains like Gundarak, where Darklords died. The ruler died, was replaced by a new ruler, then that guy died and the domain ended up becoming part of Barovia and Borca because nobody in the kingdom was able to assume the position of the new Darklord. Or Richemulot which has had a series of wererat leaders (The original Darklord, Claude Renier was murdered and replaced by his daughter, and then she was murdered and Jaqueline inherited the position of Darklord). Or Sithicus, where Lord Soth left and was replaced by Inza, the new Darklord.<br /> <br /> Now here are some Darklords who die and then come back, like Azalin, but then he's a spooky lich and coming back from death is just something liches do. And then sometimes you have weird things like OG Mordenheim (Adam is the Darklord and Mordenheim can't die as long as Adam exists). Really I think Strahd is the only exception where he can't be permanently killed, which is more due to him being the foundational cornerstone for the Demiplane of Dread and as a result he's basically a &quot;Super Vampire&quot; with some unique capabilities.</div> </div> </div> </div>Strahd<br /> Harkon Lukas<br /> Azalin<br /> Mordenheim<br /> Sir Tristan/Malken reincarnates as his own descendants<br /> Vecna (this one I give a pass)<br /> <br /> It seemed pretty common I think and Strahd is the weird case because what's the purpose of running CURSE OF STRAHD and getting the happy ending if he's going to come back?<br /> <br /> Eh, it's just my thought on it<br /> <br /> YMMV.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-04T00:17:59-07:00","id":1477994,"title":"","content":"I am currently attempting to plot out a version of WATERDEEP: DRAGON HEIST for my player characters as part of a 100+ Year campaign that starts in the Time of Troubles and each year moves a couple of decades through the Spellplague and other events. It wouldn't work normally if not for the fact the player characters are going to mostly be witnessing the canonization of all their previous Forgotten Realms games into one timeline and seeing their adventures from the outside. I think they'll enjoy that.<br /> <br /> My notes so far are:<br /> <br /> 1. Dragon Heist is basically a poorly-written (sorry) heist adventure where there's a 500K pile of gold located somewhere in Waterdeep, probably Undermountain, where a gold dragon guards it as well as a legendary mythical staff.<br /> <br /> 2. Theoretically, the adventure is supposed to be like the Curse of Strahd in that it has multiple angles to play where one of four factions: Xanathar the Beholder, Manshoon, a Asmodeus-worshiping family of snooty nobles, and Jarlaxle are looking for it. You choose one of these guys to be the bad guy and eventually find the Macguffin that leads you to the hoard.<br /> <br /> 3. The adventure is SO MUCH MORE AWESOME if you have all four of these factions trying to get the Dragon Hoard. Presumably fighting each other every bit as much as the PCs and then you have a showdown at the end where all of them are going for it like <i>The Good, the Bad, and the Ugly</i>.<br /> <br /> 4. I always play Manshoon as basically fantasy Cobra Commander and the goal of acquiring the 500K is because it will help him CONQUER THE WORLD. I actually would play him, unlike what the book suggests, as perfectly willing to cut a deal with the players. He's Lawful Evil and a man who knows that you don't last long if you don't pay your employees.<br /> <br /> 5. Xanathar the Beholder I flat out just play as Jabba the Hutt with his pet fish as his version of the Kowakian Monkey Lizard. He's fat, disgusting, and has a pit trap with an enormous monster inside it. His goal for the money is to have more money.<br /> <br /> 6. The Cassalanters (Asmodeus cultists) are pretty much like the Briarwwoods from Vox Machina. Incredibly smug unlikable assholes who DO love each other. They are trying to get their kids they promised to Asmodeus out of their deal with him, though.<br /> <br /> 7. This is a small note but if the players thwart the Cassalanters, though, Asmodeus will claim them rather than their children (who will swear Batman-like vengeance on the PCs despite their young ages). Call me old fashioned but Hell gets enough freebies so that they can't claim innocents because their parents promised them in my games. Asmodeus just heavily IMPLIED it was their children he was claiming.<br /> <br /> 8. The players will probably actually side with Jarlaxle who is like Manshoon in that he will want to make a deal with the PCs and actually abide by it. Also, he plans to give the loot back to the City of Waterdeep anyway as an enormous bribe and good will gesture.<br /> <br /> 9. The Harpers and Waterdeep want to acquire the gold for themselves. They will also be very annoyed if the PCs help Jarlaxle (let alone Manshoon). However, they're also in dire needs of funds due to the Spellplague and the fact that rebuilding civilization costs money.<br /> <br /> 10. The PCs avoiding conflict with the Gold Dragon would be best and I would have him be actually in slumber most of this time. If the PCs resolve it peacefully, which they can, Waterdeep will gain a new Lord of Waterdeep (possibly Open Lord) that will guarantee its prosperity for years to come.<br /> <br /> 11. The PCs inherit an Inn during this adventure. I would, in fact, actually have it be a brothel in Skullport because I love Skullport and it has a door leading to ANOTHER brothel in Waterdeep directly a thousand feet above it. Maybe also make it a permanent Mordekain's Magnificent Mansion and other weird qualifiers that make it somewhere they'd want to base a headquarters.<br /> <br /> 12. A Beholder, Manshoon the Archwizard, and god knows what else would be suicidal for the PCs to oppose at the climax of the adventure. Except, of course, if they had Jarlaxle or Larael or a FRIGGING GOLD DRAGON to help out. My players prefer thinking and alliances to dice rolling, though.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-04T01:00:12-07:00","id":1477996,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477991#post1477991\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Strahd<br /> Harkon Lukas<br /> Azalin<br /> Mordenheim<br /> Sir Tristan/Malken reincarnates as his own descendants<br /> Vecna (this one I give a pass)<br /> <br /> It seemed pretty common I think and Strahd is the weird case because what's the purpose of running CURSE OF STRAHD and getting the happy ending if he's going to come back?<br /> <br /> Eh, it's just my thought on it<br /> <br /> YMMV.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think those tend to exceptions. Like with Azalin, he's a lich. Killing liches is very difficult and they're prone to coming back unless you destroy their phylactery. It would be kind of odd if he were a lich without a phylactery. And then with Harkon Lukas, he simply owns a unique artifact that lets him transfer his soul when he's killed (basically like the 9th level True Mind Switch psychic power), but if you were to destroy the artifact or wrest it from Harkon's control, you'd remove his ability to survive after death.<br /> <br /> Or with Adam, upon his destruction Adam is capable of passing his essence into one of the Flesh Golems he crafted. A DM could easily run a campaign where in the course of defeating Adam, you kill all of his Flesh Golem children and then Adam, the mantel of Darklord would pass on to someone else (or the land of Lamordia has no one to claim the position and it gets divided up among other nearby domains).<br /> <br /> There's something like 50+ Darklords and the vast majority aren't immortal and could be permanently killed. In fact, with quite a few Darklords were the second or third or fourth person in their seat because their predecessors had been killed. Only a few Darklords were potentially immortal barring a specific weaknesses, but even they could be permanently destroyed. If the DM wanted to run a campaign where you kill Azalin or Harkon Lukas or whatever, it was always possible for the players to make that death permanent (it would just require more work than permanently killing a different Darklord like Jaqueline).<br /> <br /> The only real exception was Strahd due to his existence as the lynchpin of Ravenloft, and the way in which the Dark Powers made him the literal curse on Barovia itself. So long as Barovia exists or perhaps the Demiplane of Dread itself), so too does Strahd. But Strahd's true immortality was very much the exception when you look through the list of dozens and dozens of other Darklords.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-04T09:48:23-07:00","id":1478009,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477994#post1477994\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am currently attempting to plot out a version of WATERDEEP: DRAGON HEIST for my player characters as part of a 100+ Year campaign that starts in the Time of Troubles and each year moves a couple of decades through the Spellplague and other events. It wouldn't work normally if not for the fact the player characters are going to mostly be witnessing the canonization of all their previous Forgotten Realms games into one timeline and seeing their adventures from the outside. I think they'll enjoy that.<br /> <br /> My notes so far are:<br /> <br /> 1. Dragon Heist is basically a poorly-written (sorry) heist adventure where there's a 500K pile of gold located somewhere in Waterdeep, probably Undermountain, where a gold dragon guards it as well as a legendary mythical staff.<br /> <br /> 2. Theoretically, the adventure is supposed to be like the Curse of Strahd in that it has multiple angles to play where one of four factions: Xanathar the Beholder, Manshoon, a Asmodeus-worshiping family of snooty nobles, and Jarlaxle are looking for it. You choose one of these guys to be the bad guy and eventually find the Macguffin that leads you to the hoard.<br /> <br /> 3. The adventure is SO MUCH MORE AWESOME if you have all four of these factions trying to get the Dragon Hoard. Presumably fighting each other every bit as much as the PCs and then you have a showdown at the end where all of them are going for it like <i>The Good, the Bad, and the Ugly</i>.<br /> <br /> 4. I always play Manshoon as basically fantasy Cobra Commander and the goal of acquiring the 500K is because it will help him CONQUER THE WORLD. I actually would play him, unlike what the book suggests, as perfectly willing to cut a deal with the players. He's Lawful Evil and a man who knows that you don't last long if you don't pay your employees.<br /> <br /> 5. Xanathar the Beholder I flat out just play as Jabba the Hutt with his pet fish as his version of the Kowakian Monkey Lizard. He's fat, disgusting, and has a pit trap with an enormous monster inside it. His goal for the money is to have more money.<br /> <br /> 6. The Cassalanters (Asmodeus cultists) are pretty much like the Briarwwoods from Vox Machina. Incredibly smug unlikable assholes who DO love each other. They are trying to get their kids they promised to Asmodeus out of their deal with him, though.<br /> <br /> 7. This is a small note but if the players thwart the Cassalanters, though, Asmodeus will claim them rather than their children (who will swear Batman-like vengeance on the PCs despite their young ages). Call me old fashioned but Hell gets enough freebies so that they can't claim innocents because their parents promised them in my games. Asmodeus just heavily IMPLIED it was their children he was claiming.<br /> <br /> 8. The players will probably actually side with Jarlaxle who is like Manshoon in that he will want to make a deal with the PCs and actually abide by it. Also, he plans to give the loot back to the City of Waterdeep anyway as an enormous bribe and good will gesture.<br /> <br /> 9. The Harpers and Waterdeep want to acquire the gold for themselves. They will also be very annoyed if the PCs help Jarlaxle (let alone Manshoon). However, they're also in dire needs of funds due to the Spellplague and the fact that rebuilding civilization costs money.<br /> <br /> 10. The PCs avoiding conflict with the Gold Dragon would be best and I would have him be actually in slumber most of this time. If the PCs resolve it peacefully, which they can, Waterdeep will gain a new Lord of Waterdeep (possibly Open Lord) that will guarantee its prosperity for years to come.<br /> <br /> 11. The PCs inherit an Inn during this adventure. I would, in fact, actually have it be a brothel in Skullport because I love Skullport and it has a door leading to ANOTHER brothel in Waterdeep directly a thousand feet above it. Maybe also make it a permanent Mordekain's Magnificent Mansion and other weird qualifiers that make it somewhere they'd want to base a headquarters.<br /> <br /> 12. A Beholder, Manshoon the Archwizard, and god knows what else would be suicidal for the PCs to oppose at the climax of the adventure. Except, of course, if they had Jarlaxle or Larael or a FRIGGING GOLD DRAGON to help out. My players prefer thinking and alliances to dice rolling, though.</div> </div> </div> </div>That\u2019s what I did when I ran Dragon Heist. Had all four factions competing for the treasure. My players allied with Jarlaxle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-04T23:26:17-07:00","id":1478034,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477979#post1477979\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I prefer FR to Greyhawk because in general I like high fantasy better than low fantasy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Understandable, but I'm the opposite, I always preferred low-fantasy with moments of high fantasy being used sparingly.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477980#post1477980\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, I always felt Ravenloft had a lot of the White Wolf issues that they were terrified of player characters changing the world so they made the Darklords immortal.</div> </div> </div> </div>I actually think it is the opposite. The Dark Lords being immortal (which isn't always present in the setting) makes things safer for the GM, not the setting. With it, you can pit the PCs more easily against the Dark Lords to bring down their plans without worrying about them offing the Dark Lord in a anti-climatic way. When you're satisfied with death, you just go and make it permanent, during this time they can come again and again against the PCs, but that doesn't means their oppression remains untouched. The PCs may and should have lasting effects regardless.<br /> <br /> As far as I see it, what happens in the canon setting is that few if any great Dark Lord got offed in any way, regardless if they could get back to life or not, so you play your hand at their immortality if you want, but it didn't had an impact in the setting yet, so it could just not be true. Azalin is particularly good at demonstrating a Dark Lord being royally screwed and yet &quot;survive&quot; only through more common D&amp;D plot shenanigans.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477991#post1477991\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Strahd<br /> Harkon Lukas<br /> Azalin<br /> Mordenheim<br /> Sir Tristan/Malken reincarnates as his own descendants<br /> Vecna (this one I give a pass)</div> </div> </div> </div>Not so common even getting this list at face value, since it is just 6 of them, but...<br /> <br /> <b>Strahd</b> is only described as always returning in 5th edition. Originally it was just assumed that I6 Ravenloft ended with the party loosing and Gryphon Hill is only partially accepted as canon anyway.<br /> <br /> <b>Azalin </b>is as mortal as any Lich can be. Actually he managed to screw himself more than most of them, just not in permanent ways. His Phylactery is well defined and established in the setting even if virtually no one knows what it is in-setting. It's hard to break, but not impossible.<br /> <br /> <b>Tristen/Malken</b> never reincarnated, this is a <b>theory</b>, and only that. It was brought up because <b>Tristen</b>'s curse is inheritable, but more importantly than that, <b>Malken</b> is the Dark Lord currently closest to face death by old age.<br /> <br /> <b>Vecna</b> was already a Demigod Lich when he arrived, so no point in arguing.<br /> <br /> With that, only <b>Harkon Lukas</b> and <b>Mordenheim</b> are really confirmed immortals without much caveats (although certainly with their own strings attached). That's hardly setting a standard for the number of Dark Lords in the setting, especially when we have Gundarak, Sithicus and Richemulot setting more examples than those of DLs dying.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1477994#post1477994\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">WATERDEEP: DRAGON HEIST<br /> <br /> My notes so far are:</div> </div> </div> </div>1 - 310.000.000,00 USD in today's rates, possibly tens or hundreds of times that in comparative medieval rates. Quite some money.<br /> <br /> 2 - It's an easy way to make things repeatable and somewhat unpredictable, but...<br /> <br /> 3 - Doing this is so obviously better that it's a no-brainer conclusion. Go for it!<br /> <br /> 4 - I love villains that can genuinely tempt the heroes.<br /> <br /> 7 - You're old-fashioned. While I know from where you're coming from, it doesn't necessarily means Hell get to necessarily damn the children's souls in the process. Asmodeus may have any number of uses for them that leads to other adventures for your players.<br /> <br /> 9 - My economist self is screaming and crying. I love D&amp;D, but I hate D&amp;D &quot;economy&quot;.<br /> <br /> 11 - While I love this spell, you may instead base it on the Rod of Security, but with a permanent glade and a fixed passage, and have the manor built in it.<br /> <br /> 12 - There's always the option of being smart about playing them against each other during the final showdown. The winner isn't the strongest, just the last to die/flee. Especially if you play them as prioritizing their own survival.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T09:30:15-07:00","id":1478063,"title":"","content":"Although I generally prefer low fantasy to high, the Realms are the only canon material plane setting I have any interest in. Probably because it was my introduction to D&amp;D. It's also the setting most of the PC games use, which led to me getting attached to the lore, and also I have a particular interest in FR's drow and Vhaeraun and Eilistraee are my favourite deities. I couldn't comment on 4e's quality as a gaming system because the lore changes annoyed me so much I didn't even look at it. I'm sure it's perfectly good, but... No. The setting also lives in 1370-ish DR permanently for me, seeing as 5e decided to bring the twins back, tell me they were sort-of getting along and then crush my hopes by retconning it.<br /> I have occasionally considered Ravenloft and Planescape, though I have yet to get to grips with their lore.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T10:21:52-07:00","id":1478068,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar26836_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"26836\" data-vbnamecard=\"26836\">Rhywbeth</a> <br /> \u200b<br /> Eilistraee is amazing and I admit that I frequently port her to other settings anyway, but otherwise I prefer Greyhawk's deities. Most of the &quot;default&quot; D&amp;D from setting-agnostic books previous to 4th edition isn't actually setting-agnostic, but Greyhawk. Indeed, some rules that many understand as core to the system, if not always liked, are actually Greyhawk things that other settings just decided to not change, like using Vancian magic, Cleric Domains and the role of the Alignment Chart. Famous names found in spells and items lists like Tenser, Mordenkainen, Robilar and many others are Greyhawk NPCs, and the PHB gods are from Greyhawk pantheons. So a lot of players have more contact with Greyhawk than they know, as this goes on from Artifacts to standard monster lore.<br /> <br /> Planescape mostly became absorbed as the default for planar lore and rules whenever it isn't overwritten by setting-specific stuff, so if you ever read Manual of the Planes (any edition), you already brushed Planescape and only a few specifics would be needed to get the setting's lore, mostly about Sigil and its factions (and the use of gifting Mephits as a way to send messages similar to how some people did this with flowers). A similar thing happened to Spelljammer in a lessened way (otherwise it was mostly abandoned).<br /> <br /> In such regards, Dark Sun is interesting for being a very different setting he default, and the birthplace of the standard Psionic rules if those interest you in any way or at least in some settings, along with presenting weird twists to standard presentations, like cannibal Halflings and Elves as marginalized desert runners.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-06T02:33:57-07:00","id":1478129,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rhywbeth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478063#post1478063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Although I generally prefer low fantasy to high, the Realms are the only canon material plane setting I have any interest in. Probably because it was my introduction to D&amp;D. It's also the setting most of the PC games use, which led to me getting attached to the lore, and also I have a particular interest in FR's drow and Vhaeraun and Eilistraee are my favourite deities. I couldn't comment on 4e's quality as a gaming system because the lore changes annoyed me so much I didn't even look at it. I'm sure it's perfectly good, but... No. The setting also lives in 1370-ish DR permanently for me, seeing as 5e decided to bring the twins back, tell me they were sort-of getting along and then crush my hopes by retconning it.<br /> I have occasionally considered Ravenloft and Planescape, though I have yet to get to grips with their lore.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like the Realms too, but I use 5e lore. And I love Vhaeraun and Eilistraee.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-06T02:35:52-07:00","id":1478130,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478068#post1478068\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span class=\"b-bbcode-user js-bbcode-user\">Guest</span> <br /> \u200b<br /> Eilistraee is amazing and I admit that I frequently port her to other settings anyway, but otherwise I prefer Greyhawk's deities. Most of the &amp;quot;default&amp;quot; D&amp;D from setting-agnostic books previous to 4th edition isn't actually setting-agnostic, but Greyhawk. Indeed, some rules that many understand as core to the system, if not always liked, are actually Greyhawk things that other settings just decided to not change, like using Vancian magic, Cleric Domains and the role of the Alignment Chart. Famous names found in spells and items lists like Tenser, Mordenkainen, Robilar and many others are Greyhawk NPCs, and the PHB gods are from Greyhawk pantheons. So a lot of players have more contact with Greyhawk than they know, as this goes on from Artifacts to standard monster lore.<br /> <br /> Planescape mostly became absorbed as the default for planar lore and rules whenever it isn't overwritten by setting-specific stuff, so if you ever read Manual of the Planes (any edition), you already brushed Planescape and only a few specifics would be needed to get the setting's lore, mostly about Sigil and its factions (and the use of gifting Mephits as a way to send messages similar to how some people did this with flowers). A similar thing happened to Spelljammer in a lessened way (otherwise it was mostly abandoned).<br /> <br /> In such regards, Dark Sun is interesting for being a very different setting he default, and the birthplace of the standard Psionic rules if those interest you in any way or at least in some settings, along with presenting weird twists to standard presentations, like cannibal Halflings and Elves as marginalized desert runners.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually I heard from a guy who knows someone close to WOTC that Spelljammer\u2019s coming back later this year.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-16T00:11:45-07:00","id":1478938,"title":"","content":"In my fossilized case, I played AD&amp;D 1, 2, and 2.5. I switched over to oWoD completely once D&amp;D 3rd came out. Vampire, Kindred of the East, and Exalted were my main games.<br /> <br /> To me Exalted was always a better AD&amp;D for my tastes than D&amp;D 3rd/4th/5th was. I was never interested in being the murder hobos who explored the ancient ruins of the godlike civilization, instead I preferred to play the characters who built said godlike civilization in ancient history.<br /> <br /> Regardless of edition WoD in general has always presented a lower barrier for entry, and simpler mechanics which means you can focus on game play rather than rules lawyering. The fact that you can open any real world history book and have it double as a setting book means any player can google a time period or culture without having to buy a game supplement.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-16T08:58:16-07:00","id":1478956,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478938#post1478938\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my fossilized case, I played AD&amp;D 1, 2, and 2.5. I switched over to oWoD completely once D&amp;D 3rd came out. Vampire, Kindred of the East, and Exalted were my main games.<br /> <br /> To me Exalted was always a better AD&amp;D for my tastes than D&amp;D 3rd/4th/5th was. I was never interested in being the murder hobos who explored the ancient ruins of the godlike civilization, instead I preferred to play the characters who built said godlike civilization in ancient history.<br /> <br /> Regardless of edition WoD in general has always presented a lower barrier for entry, and simpler mechanics which means you can focus on game play rather than rules lawyering. The fact that you can open any real world history book and have it double as a setting book means any player can google a time period or culture without having to buy a game supplement.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes but D&amp;D 5e has much more streamlined mechanics and much more emphasis on RP than previous editions. It\u2019s just a happier more optimistic game than WoD, plus (and this has nothing to do with you guys) I just feel like the friends I play 5e with are more \u201cmy tribe\u201d than the ones I play WoD with.<br /> <br /> I do still really like WoD both WoD20 and WoD5 (right now only V5 has been released but hopefully that will change soon). I just prefer D&amp;D 5e, but that\u2019s only my opinion and I wasn\u2019t even sure about that until I posted this question and hashed it out by talking to people.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-16T09:31:26-07:00","id":1478960,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478938#post1478938\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Regardless of edition WoD in general has always presented a lower barrier for entry, and simpler mechanics which means you can focus on game play rather than rules lawyering. The fact that you can open any real world history book and have it double as a setting book means any player can google a time period or culture without having to buy a game supplement.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just to point out, D&amp;D 5th is so streamlined I'd argue it is actually easier to learn than WoD. I have one such test group currently, in my current Changeling game I have two players that never played any before and recently played both, and they found D&amp;D 5th easier to learn and start playing.<br /> <br /> Settings too, fictional settings do not need to be fully presented, or even fully developed to begin play, and have the benefit of no RL History having to be followed or Suspension of Disbelief being threatened by contradicting RL.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-16T14:17:47-07:00","id":1478974,"title":"","content":"It's hard for me to imagine most editions of D&amp;D as having significant barrier for entry. The base d20 mechanic is very simple, you don't have to memorize most of the math behind the scenes as a new player because there's a chart that just tells you what to put on your sheet and then you know what numbers to put together.<br /> <br /> For a group that's not going optimization happy, and not including a lot of supplements and thus avoiding things like Feat bloat, jumping in to D&amp;D is generally very easy (but 4e and 5e are even more so).<br /> <br /> What happens to D&amp;D though, is how rapidly the complexity shoot up after you get into it. This is obvious in the mechanics if you just do a simple exercise of create a Level 1 character from scratch vs. create a Level 5 character from scratch. But this is also true of the setting. Level 1 characters don't know much about their own fantasy worlds, so their players don't have to know much either (in most editions of D&amp;D, without a Take 10 even a maxed out knowledge character is still going to fail DC 10+ Know X checks more than not). It's highly unlikely you need to read more than 5 pages worth of material to jump into a D&amp;D game meant to start at level 1, and then the players can learn the setting as their characters explore it. Standard D&amp;D setting tropes are also very well known to a global point; lots of video games (a much larger entertainment industry) draw on things lifted off of D&amp;D so there's a familiarity there even for non-TTRPG folks to start with.<br /> <br /> The WoD games are somewhat of a flatter curve here. This means there is generally more to digest at start. Even with the real world setting to fall back on, the supernatural societies within it have huge amounts of detail about them in various books. Individual WoD factions frequently have as much text devoted to them as whole continents in D&amp;D settings. A starting level WoD character is generally more work than a starting level D&amp;D character; esp. because of the more free form char-gen process. But things don't ramp up nearly as much. Making a pure starting level WoD character vs. one with 100 XP to spend after creation (again, esp. for non-optimization focused play) isn't going to have the same feeling of a massive leap in character complexity.<br /> <br /> IME, D&amp;D has the lower bar for entry, but a higher bar for playing for more than a few sessions. D&amp;D 5e has really tried to reduce that as much as possible, but there's limits to possible without running into the 4e &quot;this doesn't feel like D&amp;D!&quot; complaints. The WoD games have generally had a higher bar for entry, but smooth out better once you're past that","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-16T16:38:39-07:00","id":1478981,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478960#post1478960\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just to point out, D&amp;D 5th is so streamlined I'd argue it is actually easier to learn than WoD. I have one such test group currently, in my current Changeling game I have two players that never played any before and recently played both, and they found D&amp;D 5th easier to learn and start playing..</div> </div> </div> </div>I take it you don't run your games in such a way as to face palm when players want to take pole arms into a dense forest or sewer tunnels, because none of them have any idea of the size of their own equipment. A bad case of ichigo slashing the hospital ceiling sort of thing. Conversely very few if any people are going to argue the dimensions of a rifle or hand gun. Likewise most urban kids have no idea how big a horse is, nevermind them getting into the &quot;how many hands high&quot; and &quot;how many stones&quot; rating systems. Yes that is specific, but at the same time it is no different than saying a honda civic will fit through a gap but a garbage truck won't.<br /> <br /> Also after so many decades of playing various RPGs, I have some issues with the standard fantasy genre setup. Adventurers don't really make sense thematically or in setting in most cases. WoD is just our world except a bit darker, a bit more corrupt, a bit less hopeful. It just has random supernatural stuff hidden in the corners.<br /> <br /> Most D&amp;D is set up in the reverse, it centers around the characters, regardless if the setting, the civilizations, economics, or societies make sense. D&amp;D is the MMORPG design where you interact with the NPCs, hand in quests, and spend your time level and loot grinding. Now can a DM take D&amp;D in a different direction, of course, but it progressively becomes more and more homebrew patching things than what the core game provides.<br /> <br /> I am not saying that all games have to be &quot;Call of D&amp;D&quot; or &quot;Historical Accuracy Simulator 2023 edition&quot;, but it is worth noting exactly how much stuff is getting handwaved which would drastically change the flavor of the games in question.<br /> <br /> The easiest system I have ever taught people was the Lone Wolf Multiplayer system, based off of the Lone Wolf choose your own adventure books by Joe Dever. A single D10 for all rolls, no XP to track just adventures completed, and an inbuilt inventory management setup to avoid the meme of where did that 10' ladder come from. Sure the characters max out in 15 - 20 games but in truth a lot of groups break up or get board before that point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-16T16:43:47-07:00","id":1478982,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478974#post1478974\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's hard for me to imagine most editions of D&amp;D as having significant barrier for entry. The base d20 mechanic is very simple, you don't have to memorize most of the math behind the scenes as a new player because there's a chart that just tells you what to put on your sheet and then you know what numbers to put together.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh you sweet summer child.<br /> <br /> THAC0 tables used to be routinely programmed into graphing calculators for ease of use. Bonus points if you have ever seen a slide rule used to calculate combat results..... I will show myself out now, the rest home called and told me I needed to get back before four o'clock dinner.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-16T17:32:24-07:00","id":1478986,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478974#post1478974\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's hard for me to imagine most editions of D&amp;D as having significant barrier for entry. The base d20 mechanic is very simple, you don't have to memorize most of the math behind the scenes as a new player because there's a chart that just tells you what to put on your sheet and then you know what numbers to put together.<br /> <br /> For a group that's not going optimization happy, and not including a lot of supplements and thus avoiding things like Feat bloat, jumping in to D&amp;D is generally very easy (but 4e and 5e are even more so).<br /> <br /> What happens to D&amp;D though, is how rapidly the complexity shoot up after you get into it. This is obvious in the mechanics if you just do a simple exercise of create a Level 1 character from scratch vs. create a Level 5 character from scratch. But this is also true of the setting. Level 1 characters don't know much about their own fantasy worlds, so their players don't have to know much either (in most editions of D&amp;D, without a Take 10 even a maxed out knowledge character is still going to fail DC 10+ Know X checks more than not). It's highly unlikely you need to read more than 5 pages worth of material to jump into a D&amp;D game meant to start at level 1, and then the players can learn the setting as their characters explore it. Standard D&amp;D setting tropes are also very well known to a global point; lots of video games (a much larger entertainment industry) draw on things lifted off of D&amp;D so there's a familiarity there even for non-TTRPG folks to start with.<br /> <br /> The WoD games are somewhat of a flatter curve here. This means there is generally more to digest at start. Even with the real world setting to fall back on, the supernatural societies within it have huge amounts of detail about them in various books. Individual WoD factions frequently have as much text devoted to them as whole continents in D&amp;D settings. A starting level WoD character is generally more work than a starting level D&amp;D character; esp. because of the more free form char-gen process. But things don't ramp up nearly as much. Making a pure starting level WoD character vs. one with 100 XP to spend after creation (again, esp. for non-optimization focused play) isn't going to have the same feeling of a massive leap in character complexity.<br /> <br /> IME, D&amp;D has the lower bar for entry, but a higher bar for playing for more than a few sessions. D&amp;D 5e has really tried to reduce that as much as possible, but there's limits to possible without running into the 4e &amp;quot;this doesn't feel like D&amp;D!&amp;quot; complaints. The WoD games have generally had a higher bar for entry, but smooth out better once you're past that</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> says overall.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-16T17:33:45-07:00","id":1478987,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478956#post1478956\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yes but D&amp;D 5e has much more streamlined mechanics and much more emphasis on RP than previous editions.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have to call bullshit there. No edition of D&amp;D has had more emphasis on RP than any other. The rules for social interaction in D&amp;D have also been rough-hewn and cumbersome affairs that fail to cover most situations beyond trying to convince monsters to step aside.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-16T17:36:07-07:00","id":1478989,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478987#post1478987\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I have to call bullshit there. No edition of D&amp;D has had more emphasis on RP than any other. The rules for social interaction in D&amp;D have also been rough-hewn and cumbersome affairs that fail to cover most situations beyond trying to convince monsters to step aside.</div> </div> </div> </div>I disagree. D&amp;D 5e is much more RP friendly than previous editions. This is a good thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-16T17:49:02-07:00","id":1478991,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478989#post1478989\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I disagree. D&amp;D 5e is much more RP friendly than previous editions. This is a good thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>How? It has the same emphasis on combat and meager, meatless bone tossed to socially-centered characters that's been the standard since AD&amp;D. It's rather telling that the answer that it's had since 3e to Charisma not getting emphasized is to make it a spellcasting ability for bards, sorcerers, and then warlocks, and then tack social interaction skills onto them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-16T19:08:52-07:00","id":1478995,"title":"","content":"Social interaction skills are much more prominent in 5e and are used a lot more. Also 5e has an extensive background system which really encourages people to RP and to develop an extensive backstory for their characters. And everyone I\u2019ve talked to who has read both 5e and earlier editions agrees that 5e is much more inclusive and RP friendly.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-16T21:06:59-07:00","id":1478998,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478981#post1478981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I take it you don't run your games in such a way</div> </div> </div> </div>First of all I must say it is becoming really, really annoying this habit of your of starting any counter-argument making assumptions about other people's experience in a condescending tone. I'm certain that a lot of people here are as sure that they know more than you as you are that you know more than anyone who disagrees with you, but we still make an effort to suppress our arrogance and treat you with due respect, please offer the same kindness in return.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478981#post1478981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">when players want to take pole arms into a dense forest or sewer tunnels, because none of them have any idea of the size of their own equipment.</div> </div> </div> </div>For your information yes, I do. I'm the kind of GM that likes to learn and understand how things really work (or worked) and includes what I learn in the setting, including descriptions, and like to discuss the matter with players. <b>The same way I argue with them the bullshit assumptions in WoD equipment charts and weapon rules, and that garrotes aren't decapitation tools</b>. Being based in the real world (a place where polearms actually exist) never stopped WoD from having a huge load of stupid assumptions both built within the game or just assumed by players.<br /> <br /> But that put, what this have to do with what I argued at all? I didn't argued that picturing the setting is easier if the setting is based directly on our world. It is. Although not because of wrong assumptions, but simply because in <i>modern </i>WoD I don't have to explain how the daily life of a common person works or which countries exist (although, as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> pointed, I usually have to explain a lot more than in D&amp;D about the cultures and politics of the fictional splats).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478981#post1478981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">nevermind them getting into the &quot;how many hands high&quot; and &quot;how many stones&quot; rating systems.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, as most US RPGs, both use a fading measure system with medieval units, that is kept alive just because people are used to it and some even see more rational systems as akin to witchcraft. So, as a person that never saw a non-metric system outside a museum or work of fiction until I learned English, I can say that this particular here actually works best for me in D&amp;D, where the old Imperial units help to acclimate to the aesthetics of pseudo-medieval. In modern WoD it is just inconvenient.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478981#post1478981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also after so many decades of playing various RPGs, I have some issues with the standard fantasy genre setup.</div> </div> </div> </div>Never argued that. You made it clear in your previous post and it is an absolutely valid reason to not play any game you associate with a fictional style you don't like. I must say that D&amp;D already has venues outside that paradigm for those with similar issues, like myself, but it is still the strong point of most D&amp;D settings, so whatever.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478981#post1478981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Most D&amp;D is set up in the reverse, it centers around the characters, regardless if the setting, the civilizations, economics, or societies make sense.</div> </div> </div> </div>No fictional setting is different from this, and WoD isn't the nice and refreshing exception. The setting is made to accommodate the stories to be told and the protagonists to be portrayed. WoD has many developments that I don't think make a lot of sense from an organic setting standpoint, but are there for the benefit of the game.<br /> <br /> Other than that, if you think fantasy settings, both D&amp;D and others, have no development beyond what the PCs will directly interact with, then I really believe you've been completely out of touch with fantasy as a genre for the last three decades and are arguing as if nothing after AD&amp;D could be any different from AD&amp;D.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478981#post1478981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now can a DM take D&amp;D in a different direction, of course, but it progressively becomes more and more homebrew patching things than what the core game provides.</div> </div> </div> </div>What? None of those things has anything to do with rules, how would that be solved through homebrewing? Even when the settings are as limited as you say (which they increasingly aren't), this takes no change in rules at all to fix.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478981#post1478981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am not saying that all games have to be &quot;Call of D&amp;D&quot; or &quot;Historical Accuracy Simulator 2023 edition&quot;, but it is worth noting exactly how much stuff is getting handwaved which would drastically change the flavor of the games in question.</div> </div> </div> </div>As much as in any other game, since every fiction handwaves things that aren't relevant to the story being told and TTRPGs are meant to be played by humans, not supercomputers. WoD does just as much handwaving, the only advantage in this regard being exactly that it can also handwave basic worldbuilding almost entirely.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478991#post1478991\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It has the same emphasis on combat and meager, meatless bone tossed to socially-centered characters that's been the standard since AD&amp;D. It's rather telling that the answer that it's had since 3e to Charisma not getting emphasized is to make it a spellcasting ability for bards, sorcerers, and then warlocks, and then tack social interaction skills onto them.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'll call bullshit on this.<br /> <br /> Good mechanics for social interaction isn't the same as being best or worse for RP. It just means you have better defined dice rolls for dealing with social interactions with dice rolls. WoD isn't in the slightest a better system for RP than D&amp;D 3.5.<br /> <br /> What helps with RP is to have tools that actually help with RP, rewarding it and making it easier to incorporate. WoD does so almost exclusively by saying that not RPing is wrong and bad players that don't RP should be punished by the ST. Ok, it also has Nature for regaining Willpower, but D&amp;D has Alignment and both are equally bland in practice and full of serious problems. Also both have guidelines for the ST to award RP with XP since D&amp;D 3rd ed.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, D&amp;D 5e introduced Backgrounds and an Inspiration mechanic with associate personality traits that, while still needs some polishing, work well exactly at giving players clear directions and benefits for RP. Another game that has actual rules to encourage and ease RP is CofD 2nd ed with Aspirations and Conditions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-16T22:22:23-07:00","id":1479000,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478998#post1478998\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Good mechanics for social interaction isn't the same as being best or worse for RP.</div> </div> </div> </div>A big old second to this.<br /> <br /> The ultimate hypothetical RPG seamlessly meshes playing a character (RP) with play the system (G). Any mechanical complexity runs the risk of pulling people out of RP to focus on all the G of the complexity. It doesn't matter if it's fighting, talking, building a castle, or inventing a functional way to start a Mars colony. It's easy to run into the trap of designing fun and engaging systems for these activities that are still G focused and don't do anything to actually make RP better (or worse, but that can get pretty subjective as different people have different needs when it comes to things like immersion for RP and how their sense of immersion handles breaks for mechanics).<br /> <br /> D&amp;D having less social traits in comparison to the WoD doesn't actually mean the WoD has better RP tools - even social RP tools - than D&amp;D does. The pre-V5 the WoD social rules are rather lacking considering how much you can invest your character in being a socially adept individual. I've seen people make solid (if not necessarily compelling to me) arguments that D&amp;D's lower stress on social traits helps RP in D&amp;D because it helps reinforce the focus on the game so you know what kind of characters you're going to be playing than a more open ended system does. They argue that the WoD's plethora of social traits is actually a trap that makes you think it's even return on investment to invest on social things as physical things, when the WoD doesn't actually do that, while D&amp;D's obvious skew towards physical traits is telling you what's the most important thing to invest in for the game side of things rather than getting bogged down on stuff that looks like it will matter to role-playing when it actually doesn't do much in that regard. Keeping in mind that this argument isn't really arguing either system is good for encouraging RP, just that D&amp;D gets out of the way better.<br /> <br /> As noted, more recent systems have done a lot of work to try to actually mesh RP and G more fully. None of them do it perfectly, and frequently many are built to very specific tastes rather than trying to have a broader appeal, but design continues to iterate and innovate new tools.<br /> <br /> But to me at least, listening to debates about whether any D&amp;D or classic WoD mechanics are better for RP is like listening to people trying to debate whether professional baseball players or professional cricket players would make for better professional American football players while both sides refuse to even acknowledge that rugby exists.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-17T00:13:25-07:00","id":1479002,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478998#post1478998\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">First of all I must say it is becoming really, really annoying this habit of your of starting any counter-argument making assumptions about other people's experience in a condescending tone.</div> </div> </div> </div>The only condescension is that which you are projecting into my words.<br /> <br /> Perhaps you would like to try the Socratic method of discourse rather than firing broadsides of opinions?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478998#post1478998\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm certain that a lot of people here are as sure that they know more than you as you are that you know more than anyone who disagrees with you, but we still make an effort to suppress our arrogance and treat you with due respect, please offer the same kindness in return.</div> </div> </div> </div>Anyone of those people are free to PM me and discuss such things at length if they have a problem or would like clarification on a given point.<br /> <br /> Respect beyond a basic level is earned rather than to be expected, but that is just my personal values at play.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-17T03:10:15-07:00","id":1479004,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479002#post1479002\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Perhaps you would like to try the Socratic method of discourse rather than firing broadsides of opinions?</div> </div> </div> </div>The premise of the thread is to voice our opinions on a subject. If you want people to engage in a Socratic dialogue where they consent to you taking the role of questioning interlocutor to their defending interlocutor, you might want to brush up on your argumentation a bit. For example, there's not much insight to be drawn from a challenge to the statement, &quot;most editions of D&amp;D don't have a significant barrier for entry for new players,&quot; to simply be, &quot;but THAC0, lol.&quot;<br /> <br /> Since this fails at even the most basic principles of Socratic debate: actually being a logically sound point. Is THAC0 the default in most editions of D&amp;D? Well..<br /> <br /> Original D&amp;D didn't use THAC0 at all, though the roots of THAC0 were present in the to-hit table. Though, the to-hit table is a DM tool, not something the players consult.<br /> <br /> AD&amp;D 1e, as well as Holmes, BX, and Rules Cyclopedia, used the formalized THAC0 rules that simplified the original oD&amp;D table to something that could be calculated as a single formula, but kept is as a DM tool. Player announced what they rolled, and the DM used their THAC0 including hit tables to see if the player rolled high enough.<br /> <br /> AD&amp;D 2e, 2e Revised, and BECMI, actually moved THAC0 to a player facing mechanic.<br /> <br /> 3e, 3.5e, 4e, Essentials, and 5e all converted to the ascending AC that's now the default and don't use THAC0 in any fashion.<br /> <br /> However you'd like to count &quot;editions&quot; here with the basic vs advanced split, and where or not to count 2.5, 3.5, and Essentials as separate editions, the numbers never come out to a refutation that most editions of D&amp;D didn't make players calculate THAC0: either is was a DM calculation or it was eliminated.<br /> <br /> So, the most basic goal of a Socratic dialogue's challenge of a hypothesis has failed: you haven't offered up a counter point that disproves the hypothesis. Let alone offer a counter that allows for the exploration of why the original hypothesis failed and how to build up a strong one in its place. Which is, lets recall, the goal of the Socratic method: to refine philosophic hypotheses until the questioner has reached a point of inability to successfully challenge the current hypothesis to refine it further.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Anyone of those people are free to PM me and discuss such things at length if they have a problem or would like clarification on a given point. </div> </div> </div>For the sake of anyone unaware:<br /> <br /> 1) PMs are still subject to the forum rules.<br /> 2) The moderators have, repeatedly, stated that if we have a problem with another user, it's a violation of the rules to take it to that user instead of asking for the moderators to moderate the issue at hand.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Respect beyond a basic level... </div> </div> </div>That's the &quot;due respect&quot; monteparnas is talking about.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-17T03:11:27-07:00","id":1479005,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479000#post1479000\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> A big old second to this.<br /> <br /> The ultimate hypothetical RPG seamlessly meshes playing a character (RP) with play the system (G). Any mechanical complexity runs the risk of pulling people out of RP to focus on all the G of the complexity. It doesn't matter if it's fighting, talking, building a castle, or inventing a functional way to start a Mars colony. It's easy to run into the trap of designing fun and engaging systems for these activities that are still G focused and don't do anything to actually make RP better (or worse, but that can get pretty subjective as different people have different needs when it comes to things like immersion for RP and how their sense of immersion handles breaks for mechanics).<br /> <br /> D&amp;D having less social traits in comparison to the WoD doesn't actually mean the WoD has better RP tools - even social RP tools - than D&amp;D does. The pre-V5 the WoD social rules are rather lacking considering how much you can invest your character in being a socially adept individual. I've seen people make solid (if not necessarily compelling to me) arguments that D&amp;D's lower stress on social traits helps RP in D&amp;D because it helps reinforce the focus on the game so you know what kind of characters you're going to be playing than a more open ended system does. They argue that the WoD's plethora of social traits is actually a trap that makes you think it's even return on investment to invest on social things as physical things, when the WoD doesn't actually do that, while D&amp;D's obvious skew towards physical traits is telling you what's the most important thing to invest in for the game side of things rather than getting bogged down on stuff that looks like it will matter to role-playing when it actually doesn't do much in that regard. Keeping in mind that this argument isn't really arguing either system is good for encouraging RP, just that D&amp;D gets out of the way better.<br /> <br /> As noted, more recent systems have done a lot of work to try to actually mesh RP and G more fully. None of them do it perfectly, and frequently many are built to very specific tastes rather than trying to have a broader appeal, but design continues to iterate and innovate new tools.<br /> <br /> But to me at least, listening to debates about whether any D&amp;D or classic WoD mechanics are better for RP is like listening to people trying to debate whether professional baseball players or professional cricket players would make for better professional American football players while both sides refuse to even acknowledge that rugby exists.</div> </div> </div> </div>I\u2019m not sure I understand your last paragraph, but when we play 5e we have a lot of RP in our games. Just as much as when I play WoD with my other friends.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-17T03:24:16-07:00","id":1479006,"title":"","content":"That last paragraph is basically a more complicated version of &quot;it's an apples vs. oranges&quot; comparison, but D&amp;D and the oWOD are both apples, the orange is probably something like Monsterhearts, and one of the versions of Cortex is a grapefruit wondering how it's being left out of all this (rugby in my initial analogy).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-17T04:42:17-07:00","id":1479007,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479004#post1479004\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The premise of the thread is to voice our opinions on a subject. If you want people to engage in a Socratic dialogue where they consent to you taking the role of questioning interlocutor to their defending interlocutor, you might want to brush up on your argumentation a bit.</div> </div> </div> </div>Socratic method involves asking questions, rather than just doing the equivalent of saying &quot;you're wrong&quot; and sticking ones tongue out. In other words if you disagree about something, you ask about a point of contention to make sure you are understanding correctly, rather than default prepping high horses and or soap boxes. <br /> <br /> Person 1: My subjective opinion<br /> Person 2: Interesting, how do you account for A, B, and C?<br /> Person 1: A is countered by D and B isn't an issue with this technique, C hasn't really come up so off the top of my head something along the lines of E<br /> Person 2: Ah but what about G?<br /> <br /> Hence the asking of questions rather than &quot;arrggh, how dare your opinion differ&quot;. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479004#post1479004\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's the &quot;due respect&quot; monteparnas is talking about.</div> </div> </div> </div>If that is the case, then Monte doesn't meet her own criteria for base civility, as evidenced by the fact that her posts often seem to go from &quot;I disagree&quot; to &quot;how dare you&quot; levels of annoyance or aggravation, often within one post and not waiting for clarification on the others part.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-17T11:36:37-07:00","id":1479022,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479007#post1479007\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Socratic method involves asking questions, rather than just doing the equivalent of saying &quot;you're wrong&quot; and sticking ones tongue out.</div> </div> </div> </div>While the Socratic method does rely on asking questions, the point is not to simply poke holes and nitpick. Proper Socratic Method should not just be engaging in JAQing off style rhetoric where questions are used to disguise the intended meaning of, &quot;you're wrong,&quot; or sea-lioning, or other such ways in which the end result is simply causing annoyance and aggravation in those you're engaging with. Even Socrates took it too far and ended up being forced to drink poison for pissing people off instead of helping them by failing to temper his questions to his audience.<br /> <br /> Again, the point of the Socratic method is ultimately constructive. Socratic dialogues are meant to strengthen the original statement by helping to refine weak points in its premises, phrasing, and logic. An ideal outcome is that the defending interlocutor feels fulfilled by the experience because the process resulted in their original statement being stronger and their own beliefs more firmly held now that they can be expressed with greater nuance; their life is no longer unexamined. A good challenging interlocutor needs to be able to ask extremely purposeful questions because finding flaws is the means, not the end. Finding flaws is to eliminate the flaws, and thus refine the opening hypothesis. They also need to be able to offer alternative hypotheses or premises to address the issues at hand in a different way.<br /> <br /> There's a reason (mostly Aristotle) that the scientific method draws heavily from the Socratic method in its base philosophic concept of how to arrive at the best answer - that is the least flawed answer of the current theory we are best capable of supporting, with scientific consensus replacing aporia - rather than necessarily aiming for perfect truths. Einsteinian physics replaced Newtonian physics not just by asking a question Newtonian physics couldn't address, but also addressing why Newtonian physics seemed right for so long (namely that Newtonian physics are right at the scale human senses normal inhabit and its only after leaving those limits do Einsteinian physics really start to differ).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In other words if you disagree about something, you ask about a point of contention to make sure you are understanding correctly, rather than default prepping high horses and or soap boxes. </div> </div> </div>That's not Socratic method, that's just asking for clarity. Conflating the two is bad rhetoric. While asking for clarity is certainly something people engaging in Socratic dialogue should do, it is not, by itself, sufficient to call your rhetoric Socratic method. Yet again, questions in the Socratic method have an explicit end goal to why you're pursuing a line of questioning.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Person 1: My subjective opinion<br /> Person 2: Interesting, how do you account for A, B, and C?<br /> Person 1: A is countered by D and B isn't an issue with this technique, C hasn't really come up so off the top of my head something along the lines of E<br /> Person 2: Ah but what about G? </div> </div> </div>This is an excellent example of poor Socratic dialogue, and a good way towards hemlock instead of aporia. Most of the Socratic school of philosophers came to the opinion that it's better to not get into a Socratic dialogue and just accept premature aporia (or as we'd say today, &quot;just agree to disagree&quot;), than to get so wrapped up in asking questions that one loses sight of the goal of the method (&quot;Ah but what about G?&quot; is a strong sign Person 2 is not engaging in Socratic thinking and is instead caught up in the urge to tear down without the imperative to rebuild).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-17T16:57:06-07:00","id":1479040,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1478998#post1478998\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">RP mechanics discussion</div> </div> </div> </div>But D&amp;D still hasn't changed particularly much about RP, so 5e still isn't really appreciably better in that respect for RP than past editions. In fact...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On the other hand, D&amp;D 5e introduced Backgrounds and an Inspiration mechanic with associate personality traits that, while still needs some polishing, work well exactly at giving players clear directions and benefits for RP. Another game that has actual rules to encourage and ease RP is CofD 2nd ed with Aspirations and Conditions. </div> </div> </div>5e didn't introduce Backgrounds. 4e did (alongside Themes, which 5e doesn't use). In fact, if one likens Backgrounds to the Secondary Skills of 1e and 2e, 3e is the only edition without them. Also, not a big player of 5e, but there seem to be some issue with people recalling that Inspiration exists and that it can be used in the community, as there are several videos on that very subject on YT.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-17T19:28:33-07:00","id":1479053,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479040#post1479040\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But D&amp;D still hasn't changed particularly much about RP, so 5e still isn't really appreciably better in that respect for RP than past editions.</div> </div> </div> </div>What do you call &quot;<i>changing much about RP</i>&quot;? What do you consider enough of a change? Or enough RP?<br /> <br /> For me 3 things make an appreciable difference in 5th edition, two of them being the aforementioned mechanics and the other being it being sufficiently simpler in comparison to 3rd edition that I feel free to mix stuff without worrying about unintended consequences down the line in my sheet. This is pretty big to me on a comparison limited to D&amp;D editions instead of D&amp;D and other games.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479040#post1479040\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">5e didn't introduce Backgrounds.</div> </div> </div> </div>It introduced what makes Backgrounds relevant for RP, which isn't skill choices or bonuses, those are merely mechanical benefits associated to having a character backstory, which may help RP but isn't much different from a character class or alignment in this regard.<br /> <br /> What makes 5th ed BGs interesting for RP are the Features that give you direct benefits to interact with NPCs and the world around you in a manner consistent with your social role. Instead of mechanical bonuses, they give you a clear and reliable advantage in the context of the story.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479040#post1479040\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, not a big player of 5e, but there seem to be some issue with people recalling that Inspiration exists and that it can be used in the community, as there are several videos on that very subject on YT.</div> </div> </div> </div>As I said, those rules need polishing. Inspiration is known for giving a lackluster benefit that becomes ever less relevant as you gain levels.<br /> <br /> It doesn't change that it is a mechanic to reward actual RP and do so in a very clear and easy to understand fashion. Indeed, you'll see most hacks on Inspiration revolving around its benefits instead of in how it is gained, because how it is gained already works pretty well as both a reward and guide for RP.<br /> <br /> But at the end of the day this is a comparison between D&amp;D editions, so I ask again, what exactly do you want out of it that 5th edition can't even be called fairly better than other editions on this particular? If it is the lack of focus on social abilities and rolls, I'll argue again that it isn't RP, it is having mechanics for <i>rolling</i> <i>social encounters</i>. Something WoD has because it is a political game (although a bad one at that where mechanics are concerned), but ultimately is about solving political challenges through dice and sheet, not RP. If it is something else, I'm yet to understand what is your point. It may very well be something that D&amp;D really isn't prepared to address yet.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-19T22:07:55-07:00","id":1479161,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479053#post1479053\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> But at the end of the day this is a comparison between D&amp;D editions, so I ask again, what exactly do you want out of it that 5th edition can't even be called fairly better than other editions on this particular?</div> </div> </div> </div>Oddly enough, it has nothing to do with the social rules as such. Rather, as D&amp;D is fundamentally a hot patch on a 1970s wargame, I would like for it to stop acting like it isn't, because trying otherwise is just an awful lot of &quot;How do you do, fellow children?&quot; Like, you don't devote that much space, time, and attention to combat rules to not use them - which also applies to the WoD, but it doesn't have to have them, whereas most people wouldn't go to D&amp;D if it weren't about dungeon fantasy fights.<br /> <br /> 4e recognized this, and seems better for it. Themes from the same edition also helped to fill in the space that might get flattened out to Inspiration in some occasions, but remain unique to the theme in question - and you're not likely to get a mechanically fairly simple werebeast out of 5e. It seems like it wants to make it an overwrought production a la 3e level adjustment, if it allows it at all. 5e tends to lean too hard on the DM and its community for this.<br /> <br /> And, of course, there's the ridiculous need to stretch around the engine to make the centaur, among other monster PCs, medium size because of the ramifications of the combat system that don't exist prior to 3e. That's part of why the first two editions aren't really losing out to 5e in the RP department, either. Secondary Skills are also not to be discounted as a Background-alike; they may lean hard on the DM to flesh them out, but, as noted, that's not really any less of a lean than what 5e is doing about everything, including keeping track of who has Inspiration.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-20T01:00:12-07:00","id":1479165,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479161#post1479161\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Rather, as D&amp;D is fundamentally a hot patch on a 1970s wargame, I would like for it to stop acting like it isn't, because trying otherwise is just an awful lot of &quot;How do you do, fellow children?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem is it is distinctly possible for D&amp;D to be both a hot patch to a 70s wargame, and something more than that. D&amp;D isn't pretending to be something it isn't. It's trying to be the most of the thing that came out of it's origins. I could point to countless examples of different sort of games that started as a hotpatch/mod/whatever of some other game and grew into their own things. They benefit from focusing on what that new thing that made them special and stand out from ever other &quot;we tried out this different thing based on this existing engine&quot; attempt out there in the first place.<br /> <br /> What made D&amp;D stand out from other &quot;hotpatches&quot; to wargames was moving from playing the &quot;general&quot; that orders units of largely nameless individuals around the map in military conflicts, to players having one champion that they play specifically. Chainmail &quot;hotpached&quot; 1:20 scale combat by adding in 1:1 combat and a fantasy supplement.... but those alone didn't turn it isn't a different thing. It just gave your rules for two champion units facing off in a one-on-one duel instead of large scale mass combat, and then gave you rules for fantasy creatures on top of your standard Medieval wargame fare. When the original D&amp;D team realized that there was a distinct desire to play original characters over extended periods of time in 1:1 scale, they made a game that centered around just that and dumped the 1:20 stuff for the most part. Thus we got D&amp;D, which become more than just a hotpatch to Chainmail, as it became its own thing after a few iterations. Just like Counter Strike and Team Fortress aren't just Half-Life mods any more. D&amp;D developers might benefit from remembering how it got started, butt hat doesn't mean going back to 1:20 holdovers in the original D&amp;D books, it means focusing on why people wanted to play their own unique characters in a wargame based system in the first place. Team Fortress isn't wrong for getting away from beating up headcrabs with a crowbar to iterate into a greater and greater focus on each class having a unique role in a team based multiplayer FPS. That's what makes Team Fortress fun (and why it's been copied repeatedly by multiple huge FPS games), and forcing everyone to go back to smacking headcrabs with a crowbar to earn their class would be a huge mistake.<br /> <br /> Or, in other words, RP is what made D&amp;D stand out, and RP meshed with wargame is what it should be striving to do. Not striving to go back to being Chainmail with OC champion units for 1:1 scale fights.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...whereas most people wouldn't go to D&amp;D if it weren't about dungeon fantasy fights. </div> </div> </div>The plethora of RPGs that aren't dungeon fantasy fights indicates otherwise. People go to D&amp;D because D&amp;D is the biggest name in RPGs, so it seems like the obvious place to start. D&amp;D is so dominant that a mediocre sci-fi game based on D&amp;D can easily compete with if not outsell some sci-fi games lots of gamers (even D&amp;D fans) would agree is better at doing sci-fi stuff.<br /> <br /> People go to D&amp;D because they want to try RPGs, and D&amp;D seems like the place to start.<br /> <br /> If people only went to D&amp;D for the dungeon fantasy fights, the OGL boom wouldn't have happened. It happened because everyone was trying to shove every genre possible into the d20 system that was the most well known system (and the essentials of it still are) in the world. The boom's glut was horrible because you could take almost anything, kludge it into d20 shaped box, slap a d20 logo on it,and sell more then enough copies to make a profit. Even the oWW was guilty of it. Nobody would have made MC's WoD, or the d20 Engel, or d20 Trinity-verse games, if d20's only appeal was dungeon fights.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4e recognized this, and seems better for it. </div> </div> </div>Except 4e's legacy is basically, the most mechanically sound D&amp;D game, with the best GM tools, and being completely divisive over whether or not it captured the &quot;feel&quot; of D&amp;D or not.<br /> <br /> I've never seen anyone hype 4e as the &quot;roleplayer's edition of D&amp;D.&quot; It's the, &quot;GMing combat is actually fun for the GM edition of D&amp;D.&quot; Focusing on those wargame roots made for some great systems, but failed to capture that spark that made D&amp;D standout in the first place, and you don't really see WotC regretting 5e moving back to closer to 3e in how it runs for it.<br /> <br /> When I look at what games like Lancer from Masif Press are doing with the 4e combat engine and better non-combat mechanics, it's clear 4e should be considered a great game. But that doesn't mean it's a great D&amp;D game. As an edition of D&amp;D I just don't see the argument for how it was better for focusing on the wargame stuff and not on the &quot;I want to play my original character&quot; stuff successfully.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Themes from the same edition also helped to fill in the space that might get flattened out to Inspiration in some occasions, but remain unique to the theme in question - and you're not likely to get a mechanically fairly simple werebeast out of 5e. It seems like it wants to make it an overwrought production a la 3e level adjustment, if it allows it at all. 5e tends to lean too hard on the DM and its community for this.<br /> <br /> And, of course, there's the ridiculous need to stretch around the engine to make the centaur, among other monster PCs, medium size because of the ramifications of the combat system that don't exist prior to 3e. That's part of why the first two editions aren't really losing out to 5e in the RP department, either. Secondary Skills are also not to be discounted as a Background-alike; they may lean hard on the DM to flesh them out, but, as noted, that's not really any less of a lean than what 5e is doing about everything, including keeping track of who has Inspiration. </div> </div> </div>As RP tools, I can't think of any times Themes or Secondary Skills actually encouraged fun RP at the table. Inspiration was handled poorly in 5e too, but Mojo in X-Crawl (which game first but works similarly) and Fortune from Pugmire are directly linked to some of my favorite D&amp;D/d20 base RP moments of my entire gaming life. It is definitely not about the ability to add some flavor and uniqueness to your character with some mildly useful side abilities. It's about a system that actually had incentives to RP your character doing cool things instead of mechanically optimal things.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-20T08:24:41-07:00","id":1479172,"title":"","content":"I'm a bit (RPG) queer here <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> - I run both <a href=\"https://www.realmsofpugmire.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Realms of Pugmire</b></a> ( more lighthearted D&amp;D 5E, with a bit more narrative rules, with animals as PCs ) and <b>CoD</b>, now <b>Scion</b> and <b>Trinity</b>. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Love them both, depending on mood.<br /> <br /> <b>RoP</b> is much simpler than <b>CoD</b> / <b>Storypath</b> games to run and play - and is great for dungeon crawling or 'simple Sunday roleplay'. <b>Storypaths</b> are much more narrative driven. Both has pros and cons. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 04-20-2022, 11:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-20T10:11:34-07:00","id":1479175,"title":"","content":"I still love Trinity Continuum. I\u2019ve just moved away from WoD.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-20T14:03:26-07:00","id":1479195,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479165#post1479165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The problem is it is distinctly possible for D&amp;D to be both a hot patch to a 70s wargame, and something more than that. D&amp;D isn't pretending to be something it isn't. It's trying to be the most of the thing that came out of it's origins. I could point to countless examples of different sort of games that started as a hotpatch/mod/whatever of some other game and grew into their own things. They benefit from focusing on what that new thing that made them special and stand out from ever other &quot;we tried out this different thing based on this existing engine&quot; attempt out there in the first place.<br /> <br /> What made D&amp;D stand out from other &quot;hotpatches&quot; to wargames was moving from playing the &quot;general&quot; that orders units of largely nameless individuals around the map in military conflicts, to players having one champion that they play specifically. Chainmail &quot;hotpached&quot; 1:20 scale combat by adding in 1:1 combat and a fantasy supplement.... but those alone didn't turn it isn't a different thing. It just gave your rules for two champion units facing off in a one-on-one duel instead of large scale mass combat, and then gave you rules for fantasy creatures on top of your standard Medieval wargame fare. When the original D&amp;D team realized that there was a distinct desire to play original characters over extended periods of time in 1:1 scale, they made a game that centered around just that and dumped the 1:20 stuff for the most part. Thus we got D&amp;D, which become more than just a hotpatch to Chainmail, as it became its own thing after a few iterations. Just like Counter Strike and Team Fortress aren't just Half-Life mods any more. D&amp;D developers might benefit from remembering how it got started, butt hat doesn't mean going back to 1:20 holdovers in the original D&amp;D books, it means focusing on why people wanted to play their own unique characters in a wargame based system in the first place. Team Fortress isn't wrong for getting away from beating up headcrabs with a crowbar to iterate into a greater and greater focus on each class having a unique role in a team based multiplayer FPS. That's what makes Team Fortress fun (and why it's been copied repeatedly by multiple huge FPS games), and forcing everyone to go back to smacking headcrabs with a crowbar to earn their class would be a huge mistake.<br /> <br /> Or, in other words, RP is what made D&amp;D stand out, and RP meshed with wargame is what it should be striving to do. Not striving to go back to being Chainmail with OC champion units for 1:1 scale fights.</div> </div> </div> </div>And that RP probably isn't hampered or enabled one way or another across editions because it, yes, still very much leans on combat rules. RP gets treated as a potential way to avoid them, the part of the game with the most word count dedicated to it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The plethora of RPGs that aren't dungeon fantasy fights indicates otherwise. People go to D&amp;D because D&amp;D is the biggest name in RPGs, so it seems like the obvious place to start. D&amp;D is so dominant that a mediocre sci-fi game based on D&amp;D can easily compete with if not outsell some sci-fi games lots of gamers (even D&amp;D fans) would agree is better at doing sci-fi stuff.<br /> <br /> People go to D&amp;D because they want to try RPGs, and D&amp;D seems like the place to start. </div> </div> </div>Because of monopolistic business practices, yes. Unlike Xerox, holders of D&amp;D never took many pains to try and note that D&amp;D wasn't all RPGs, but they've never suffered for it the way that Xerox would have if it didn't do that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If people only went to D&amp;D for the dungeon fantasy fights, the OGL boom wouldn't have happened. It happened because everyone was trying to shove every genre possible into the d20 system that was the most well known system (and the essentials of it still are) in the world. The boom's glut was horrible because you could take almost anything, kludge it into d20 shaped box, slap a d20 logo on it,and sell more then enough copies to make a profit. Even the oWW was guilty of it. Nobody would have made MC's WoD, or the d20 Engel, or d20 Trinity-verse games, if d20's only appeal was dungeon fights. </div> </div> </div>The OGL boom that was followed by a tremendous flood of shovelware that tanked the market, further cementing the aforementioned monopolistic business practices.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Except 4e's legacy is basically, the most mechanically sound D&amp;D game, with the best GM tools, and being completely divisive over whether or not it captured the &quot;feel&quot; of D&amp;D or not. </div> </div> </div>And in mechanical soundness, you can sit back and not worry about things that might otherwise get in the way. Clerics don't have to be healbots, fighters don't have to suck in perpetuity, and the ranger actually has a coherent theme. And if people want to say that isn't keeping the feeling of D&amp;D, perhaps they'd like to have their paladin character fall and get turned into a fighter the moment that they breathe in a way that their code doesn't approve of, which apparently, according to Gygax's John Milton Chivington cheerleading, does not prevent them from massacring orc non-combatants.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I've never seen anyone hype 4e as the &quot;roleplayer's edition of D&amp;D.&quot; It's the, &quot;GMing combat is actually fun for the GM edition of D&amp;D.&quot; Focusing on those wargame roots made for some great systems, but failed to capture that spark that made D&amp;D standout in the first place, and you don't really see WotC regretting 5e moving back to closer to 3e in how it runs for it. </div> </div> </div>Except that in a game that leans so heavily on combat, the approach to combat is part of the RP, is it not? At the very least, it's a kind of character building touch to have even the basic attacks tailored to an OC as tricks that they've presumably picked up to defend themselves.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When I look at what games like Lancer from Masif Press are doing with the 4e combat engine and better non-combat mechanics, it's clear 4e should be considered a great game. But that doesn't mean it's a great D&amp;D game. As an edition of D&amp;D I just don't see the argument for how it was better for focusing on the wargame stuff and not on the &quot;I want to play my original character&quot; stuff successfully. </div> </div> </div>It had so many more options, not just in spinning Primal off to not be Divine by another name, or by letting Martial shine all around, but in the ritual casting that 5e gutted for no good reason. 5e didn't have to throw those out, but the grognards demanded a purge, so it seems.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As RP tools, I can't think of any times Themes or Secondary Skills actually encouraged fun RP at the table. Inspiration was handled poorly in 5e too, but Mojo in X-Crawl (which game first but works similarly) and Fortune from Pugmire are directly linked to some of my favorite D&amp;D/d20 base RP moments of my entire gaming life. It is definitely not about the ability to add some flavor and uniqueness to your character with some mildly useful side abilities. It's about a system that actually had incentives to RP your character doing cool things instead of mechanically optimal things. </div> </div> </div>If they let someone play something they wanted to but otherwise might not be able to, is that not enabling RP? That's why the Oath of the Ancients is akin to a dump taken on someone's dinner plate - it's not a warden, will never be anything like a warden, yet often gets sold as the closest thing to it. Ditto for the Avenger and Oath of Vengeance paladin. And then there's the conspicuous lack of the warlord/marshal... and no, the batlle master fighter isn't one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-20T16:40:16-07:00","id":1479198,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479195#post1479195\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And that RP probably isn't hampered or enabled one way or another across editions because it, yes, still very much leans on combat rules.</div> </div> </div> </div>Being a combat heavy game doesn't mean even relatively small mechanical changes can't influence how much RP is enabled by the system.<br /> <br /> Do you actually have a case here, or do you think just bombarding people with questions that toe the line of JAQing off is enough to make it look like you have one?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> RP gets treated as a potential way to avoid them, the part of the game with the most word count dedicated to it. </div> </div> </div>We're talking about different editions of D&amp;D vs. each other. It doesn't matter how much D&amp;D focuses on combat compared to other games.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because of monopolistic business practices, yes. </div> </div> </div>So, you acknowledge your original statement of, &quot;...whereas most people wouldn't go to D&amp;D if it weren't about dungeon fantasy fights...&quot; is incorrect?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And in mechanical soundness, you can sit back and not worry about things that might otherwise get in the way. </div> </div> </div>That doesn't actually mean themes encourage RP.<br /> <br /> Should I take this point to basically mean that you want to edition war for 4e, but you know well enough to obfuscate it to avoid infractions? Because I can't come up with any other way to interpret your posts at this moment.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It had so many more options, not just in spinning Primal off to not be Divine by another name, or by letting Martial shine all around, but in the ritual casting that 5e gutted for no good reason. 5e didn't have to throw those out, but the grognards demanded a purge, so it seems. </div> </div> </div>What does any of that actually have to do with RPing at the table?<br /> <br /> Again, you're just coming off as salty that your favorite D&amp;D edition got canned, and canning it was the right business move for WotC because 5e is blowing 4e out of the water in popularity, even among new players not just grognards. Becoming a 4e grognard doesn't make things 5e does right bad.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If they let someone play something they wanted to but otherwise might not be able to, is that not enabling RP? </div> </div> </div>Not inherently. Limitations are part of RP. Even if you go to different inspirations for the larger hobby, things like improv acting exercises still have rules and limitations on characters. You don't get to play anything you might want, because limiting yourself to the situation and building trust with your other actors involves cooperation minded thinking and not just worrying about what you want.<br /> <br /> Pure freeform acting is incredibly difficult for role-playing because we define roles equally by what they aren't as by what they are. If you don't know what your character's limitations are, it's hard to know how to play them because you don't know where the line of taking something too far is.<br /> <br /> You're talking to someone that started with point-buy char-gen games, not D&amp;D. The class/level system of D&amp;D is something I hated for years because I was used to being able to customize my characters with much more granularity than that base allows. As I've aged, and gained experience, and tried more thing, it's still not my preference for the most fun, but I stopped hating class/level games because I've seen how the more limited setup enables really fun games. No edition of D&amp;D is going to make my top 10 favorite RPG list, because I still don't find its general approach as fun as lots of other games (including games heavily inspired by D&amp;D on a mechanical level).<br /> <br /> Part of that perspective? Conflating character build options with RP is a mistake. It was one of the cWoD's biggest mistakes, that generated a whole generation of indie games that wanted to live up to the concept of &quot;storytelling game&quot; that VtM/WoD failed to do instead of having a massive combat chapter. Mechanics that actually encourage and reward RP are what enable RP. &quot;I get a +X to blacksmithing related checks because before I was a fighter I was a smith,&quot; doesn't enable RP no matter how you implement it. It's just another char-gen widget to get a mechanical bonus. If you get that bonus no matter how much or how little being a former blacksmith comes up in your playing of your character, it didn't enable anything.<br /> <br /> Bonuses or limitations that actually influence how your role play your character are mechanics that enable RP. Getting a more general bonus because your character gets to spend some time in a forge because they find smithing harkens back to simpler time and relaxing, enables RP. It's an encouragement to play your former blacksmith as a former blacksmith, not just remembering that detail when it matters for a bonus. It's a statement of an important emotional coping mechanism for your character that will relate to how they feel about things. Do they want to go exploring away from places with a forge for months at a time and deprive themselves that stress relief? Or do they want to seek out adventure closer to towns and cities where they can come back and put some metal to the anvil to unwind?<br /> <br /> That's the argument in favor of inspiration over things like secondary skills or themes. Inspiration isn't just an additional character widget to make your character a bit more well rounded. It's a mechanic that directly rewards you for seeking out chances to play your character's personality: their history, their strengths, their flaws, their quirks, and so on. 5e did itself a disservice by burying instead of making it an upfront major mechanic. But, again, having played games where similar mechanics are front and center? RP is better for it even in combat oriented RPGs that still wear those war game roots on their sleeves.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-20T19:37:55-07:00","id":1479208,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479195#post1479195\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And that RP probably isn't hampered or enabled one way or another across editions because it, yes, still very much leans on combat rules. RP gets treated as a potential way to avoid them, the part of the game with the most word count dedicated to it.</div> </div> </div> </div>It seems to me that half the time you're arguing that 5e isn't better for RP because you don't think it should be, under this weird assumption that RP is an alternative to combat. And the other half you just conflate RP with character conceptualization. I think we're not disagreeing as much as we're not even talking the same language.<br /> <br /> RP means interpretation, pretending to be the character and either talking or at least making decisions as the character, engaging with the role as opposed to with the challenge. Deciding to fast-talk the enemies instead of bashing them isn't RPing by itself, it is making a strategic decision. Engaging in conversation with the enemy during battle, exchanging short phrases as you try to get your point across is RPing. Or deciding to do battle anyway, when fast-talking is obviously easier and better, because this is your chance to prove something to yourself, is RPing. It is engaging with the part of the game where we are telling a story during play, and trying to convey the drama of the moment.<br /> <br /> That's why good rules on creating a werecreature or better mechanics for a Warlord aren't RP mechanics, but Inspiration, with all its problems, that just gives a small benefit <i>for dramatizing your personal quirks</i>, is.<br /> <br /> Other than that, <i>my</i> point in this tangent was just about the definition of Roleplaying. RP isn't the only aspect or the most important aspect of the game, not in WoD and not in D&amp;D, as long as it's there. 5th ed being better for RP is a fact as far as I can tell, by my observations and those of many others, but that means nothing. I know a lot of people like to brag about RP as the golden standard and most right and valid way of playing, but no one here said such bullshit (yet), literally Amethyst just stated they like it in the edition.<br /> <br /> You seem frustrated with the end of fourth edition and the path 5th took, but denying that 5th is better at <i>something</i> because you don't think it deserves praise isn't exactly the answer. It is a completely valid point on its own right to be discussed instead of confusingly conflated with another.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-21T11:52:52-07:00","id":1479253,"title":"","content":"I do prefer 5e because I feel like it\u2019s the best edition for RP and I do hate 4e (though admittedly I\u2019ve never played it), but if you prefer to use D&amp;D as a dungeon crawling game, that\u2019s totally a valid play style. It\u2019s just not one I personally enjoy in large doses.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-21T13:01:46-07:00","id":1479259,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479253#post1479253\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I do prefer 5e because I feel like it\u2019s the best edition for RP and I do hate 4e (though admittedly I\u2019ve never played it), but if you prefer to use D&amp;D as a dungeon crawling game, that\u2019s totally a valid play style. It\u2019s just not one I personally enjoy in large doses.</div> </div> </div> </div>I actually find 4th edition fine. It brought a lot of interesting mechanics to the game and while it isn't my favored one, I do think the game is all the better for what came with it. I'm less impressed with the changes it brought to pre-existing settings, but that's not so much 4th edition's fault as it was a series of specific bad decisions on lore building.<br /> <br /> My sincere suggestion: play it. Even if just to go back to 5th later, go there and play it, with mind and heart open instead of just wanting to find justification for your opinion. I've already played 4th with grognards that wanted nothing but to find flaws and bash the game, and it was a terrible experience, but playing with people open to enjoy it was actually pretty good.<br /> <br /> From my personal experience: a huge amount of resistance to 4th edition came from WotC giving the books and text too much of an MMO <i><b>aesthetics</b></i> instead of any actual problems with the rules or lore. That was a bad decision, but the game behind those aesthetics was pretty good. I think the <i>powers </i>could have made it clearer that you can adapt the description, too, but that's secondary. And I don't like their progression on equipment, but I actually think it an improvement on 3rd edition, that I didn't liked either, because what I don't like is the whole <i>idea</i> of attaching character progression to equipment, I prefer to have more freedom on equipment distribution, especially magical items.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-21T13:44:48-07:00","id":1479263,"title":"","content":"If, for various reasons, you don't feel like going back and giving 4e a try, I'd suggest Massif Press's Icon playtest as an alternative. Like Lancer, it uses a lot of the innovative design work that went into 4e, layers in some other stuff from more recent games, and is a heroic fantasy RPG that isn't trying to be D&amp;D so it can help avoid some of the tensions that come out of preconceptions like how different Classes should operate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-21T16:14:37-07:00","id":1479266,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1477121-what-do-you-like-better-d-d-5e-or-wod-and-why?p=1479195#post1479195\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> And in mechanical soundness, you can sit back and not worry about things that might otherwise get in the way. Clerics don't have to be healbots, fighters don't have to suck in perpetuity, and the ranger actually has a coherent theme. And if people want to say that isn't keeping the feeling of D&amp;D, perhaps they'd like to have their paladin character fall and get turned into a fighter the moment that they breathe in a way that their code doesn't approve of, which apparently, according to Gygax's John Milton Chivington cheerleading, does not prevent them from massacring orc non-combatants.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Eh, what?<br /> <br /> 4th was the hardest, &quot;this is the one thing your character does, hope you enjoy it&quot; style of game i've ever played<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Prone to being a Classic Curmudgeon, goshdarned whippersnappers...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1037-jamiemalk","name":"jamiemalk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1037_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"399"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}